window.CET6_BANKS.push({"id": "2025-12-2", "source": "2025年12月真题·第2套", "writing": {"prompt": "Directions: For this part, you are allowed 30 minutes to write an essay that begins with the sentence \"It is believed that teachers can exert a profound influence on their students' academic pursuit and personal development.\" You can make comments, cite examples or use your personal experiences to develop your essay. You should write at least 150 words but no more than 200 words. You should copy the sentence given in quotes at the beginning of your essay.", "sample": "It is believed that teachers can exert a profound influence on their students' academic pursuit and personal\ndevelopment. Having spent years in classrooms, I firmly agree that a dedicated teacher shapes not only what\nwe learn but also who we become.\nOn the one hand, teachers guide our academic growth. My high-school chemistry teacher, Mr. Li, turned a\nsubject I once dreaded into my favourite one. By designing vivid experiments and patiently answering my\nendless questions, he inspired me to pursue science and eventually major in it at university. On the other hand,\nteachers mould our character. Through his fairness and honesty, Mr. Li taught me to value integrity over easy\nshortcuts, a lesson that still steers my decisions today.\nIn conclusion, a good teacher enlightens the mind and cultivates the heart at the same time. Their influence,\nthough quiet and gradual, reaches far beyond examinations and often lasts a whole lifetime."}, "translation": {"prompt": "中华民族崇尚自强自立的精神，这一精神深植于中国文化之中，是民族生存与发展的重要支撑。改革开放以来，中国在自强自立的征程上，持续加大科研投入，在信息技术、人工智能、航天工程等领域不断取得新突破。5G 网络覆盖全国，远程医疗发展迅速，电子支付的使用全球领先，嫦娥探月工程展现了航天领域引人注目的发展。自强自主精神成为推动国家现代化进程的强大动力，激励着中国人民为实现民族复兴 (rejuvenation) 的中国梦不断奋进。", "sample": "The Chinese nation values the spirit of self-reliance and self-improvement, which is deeply rooted in Chinese culture and serves as a vital support for the survival and development of the nation. Since the reform and opening-up, China has continued to increase its investment in scientific research on its journey toward self-reliance, and has kept making new breakthroughs in such fields as information technology, artificial intelligence and aerospace engineering. For example, 5G networks now cover the whole country, telemedicine is developing rapidly, and electronic payment is used more widely than anywhere else in the world; the Chang'e lunar exploration program also demonstrates remarkable progress in China's aerospace field. The spirit of self-reliance and self-improvement has become a powerful driving force behind the country's modernization, inspiring the Chinese people to keep forging ahead toward the Chinese Dream of national rejuvenation."}, "listeningUrl": "https://english-exam.lazynote.cn/cet6/paper/2025-12-2/?f=w", "clozePassage": "We humans love to believe we are special, but science and technology have repeatedly proved this conviction wrong. People  __26__  thought that humans were the only animals to use tools, to form teams or to propagate culture, but science has shown that other animals do each of these things.\nMeanwhile, technology has quashed(使无效), one by one, claims that  __27__  tasks require a human brain. The first adding machine was invented in 1623. This past year, a computer-generated work won an art contest. I believe the moment when computers meet and  __28__  human intelligence is on the horizon.\nHow will human intelligence and creativity be valued when machines become smarter and more creative than the brightest people? There will likely be a spectrum. In some  __29__  , people still value humans doing things, even if a computer can do it better. It's been a quarter of a century since a computer beat world champion Garry Kasparov, but human chess hasn't gone away.\nIn other fields, human skill will seem costly and  __30__  . Take illustration for example: most readers don't care whether the graphic  __31__  a magazine article was drawn by a person or a computer — they just want it to be relevant, new and perhaps  __32__  .\nAnd, of course, this question isn't black or white. Many fields will be a  __33__  , where some humans find a lucky place, but most of the work is done by computers, like in manufacturing today, where much of the work is accomplished by robots, but some humans must be there to  __34__  the machines.\nIf history is any guide, it's almost certain that advances in AI will cause more jobs to  __35__  , that creative workers with human-only skills will become richer but fewer in number, and that those who own creative technology will become the new ultra-rich.", "matchIntro": "Do You Know When to Quit Wisely?", "matchParagraphs": [{"label": "A", "text": "Projects, relationships, and jobs don't always work out as planned — and it's not always clear when to give up. We've all repeatedly heard the saying that \"quitters never win\", but sometimes the best decision is to cut our losses."}, {"label": "B", "text": "Think about that relationship you held on to even after it had run its course or the job you stayed in despite your boss making you miserable. Many of us try to hold onto the last ray of hope, thinking we can make it work, whatever \"it\" is. And while perseverance is generally an excellent quality, it may also incline you to over-invest in something that is no longer a good idea."}, {"label": "C", "text": "As an executive coach, I work with numerous passionate and hard-working leaders who don't want to \"quit\". But the smartest leaders learn to discern the difference between quitting too soon and holding onto something that they shouldn't."}, {"label": "D", "text": "We are all susceptible to the sunk-cost delusion, an unconscious bias that leads us to persist in an endeavor we have already invested time, effort, or money into — even when abandoning it would be more beneficial. We realize that we can't get back the resources we've given, so we persevere — investing in a project that should be halted, staying in a relationship that should be over, or finishing a book we no longer enjoy and should put down, because we're already halfway through it. If quitting is \"not your thing\", you may be especially susceptible to this bias."}, {"label": "E", "text": "The sunk-cost delusion causes us to overly worry about what we'll lose if we move on and not think enough about the costs of not moving on. To offset this bias, deliberately refocus your thinking on the gains so you can more objectively weigh the alternatives. Ask yourself: What might I gain by cutting my losses now? Will you be happier or have time for another (better) opportunity? Then ask yourself: What will it cost me to soldier on? For example, is it possible that you'll be throwing more good money after what you've already lost? Or that you won't have the energy to capitalize on other promising possibilities?"}, {"label": "F", "text": "The illusion of control can also interfere with our best judgment, leading us to overestimate our ability to control events and attain a positive outcome. This bias gives us a sense of agency and can promote mental health. However, the bias can also result in escalating commitment to a losing course of action. Especially when combined with an \"I'm not a quitter\" mentality, feeling like we have more control over an event or person than we do puts us at risk of doubling down when we should pull out."}, {"label": "G", "text": "To counteract this bias, consider your situation and make a simple two-column list of what's in your control and what's not. Think rigorously. While you may be able to influence other people and various circumstances, you can't force them to change or go your way."}, {"label": "H", "text": "Getting clear on what you can control and what you can't is essential to making a quality decision about whether to call it quits or persevere. With a written list in hand, you can ensure you focus on what you can control and better assess whether your continued efforts are worth the expense. Expending effort and emotion on things you can't control can be both draining and disempowering."}, {"label": "I", "text": "Research indicates that people link their self-identity and social status to their commitments. And because we identify with our commitments, withdrawing from one can feel like a threat to our identity or status."}, {"label": "J", "text": "Take my client Ryan, a leader at a technology company. Ryan had invested two years and a lot of effort in a design project that wasn't delivering the needed outcomes, but the thought of abandoning it after all she had put in was hard to bear. As Ryan and I explored whether she should cancel the initiative she'd been leading or continue to persevere, she stated, \"The thing is, I'm not a quitter.\" This singular, fixed sense of self was clouding her judgment and limiting her choices for action."}, {"label": "K", "text": "Our self-concept and identity are mental concepts that influence our behavior. If you strongly identify as not being a quitter, you can end up staying stuck in a course of action due to your narrow and rigid self-concept. To overcome this kind of internal obstacle, recall your other positive traits and personality characteristics. Ask yourself: What additional strengths do I have (or aspire to have) that would be helpful to employ here? For example, when I asked Ryan what other positive qualities from her personality she needed to leverage for decision making, she replied, \"Discernment and bravery.\""}, {"label": "L", "text": "Now, examine your situation through the lens of each of these facets of yourself. What does the discerning part of you say? The brave part? Considering your decision through these different characteristics will enable you to see that you have more choices. It's also helpful to seek external sources of information, rather than relying solely on your thinking and instincts. But it's important to think through whose opinions you solicit."}, {"label": "M", "text": "Ideally, seek out people who are less invested in your decision than you are. If you're considering whether to shut down your entrepreneurial venture, for instance, reach out to other founders as opposed to just your business partner. Similarly, if you're looking for love advice, ask someone removed from the ups and downs of your relationship instead of relying only on your best friend. In all cases, I recommend describing your situation and following up with a question like: \"How would you manage this situation?\""}, {"label": "N", "text": "Alternately, you could also seek out hard data that illuminates your real chance of success in whatever endeavor you are contemplating. Sticking with the example of the entrepreneurial venture, numbers are your best friend. Use them to remove yourself from the love you have for this idea and consider your burn rate relative to growth plan, industry growth rates, and other factors that predict start-up success. External sources of information can help you see angles and possibilities you might not have considered."}, {"label": "O", "text": "When something that seemed so promising at the start doesn't turn out the way we imagined, we can become overly self-critical. Nobody likes to feel that they've made a poor decision, especially if making intelligent decisions is a quality you hold in high esteem. The most effective strategy here is to give yourself a break. Practicing self-compassion increases your resilience(韧性), emotional intelligence, compassion toward others, and can boost your performance as well as help you develop a growth mindset."}], "readingPassages": {"46": "As interdependent beings we cannot thrive independent of others. We like to work with people who are like-minded, with whom we share a common interest, or who also believe in a cause we are serious about. This is evident in how we have organized ourselves into various collective groups such as nations, regions, communities, organizations, and families.\nDoes the group you belong to have a strong collective consciousness or shared beliefs? Is there interdependence among its members? Do you consider this group a significant part of your existence? If your answers are affirmative, then you are experiencing a micro version of solidarity.\nWhat is solidarity? Its simplest definition in the dictionary is: \"… a unifying opinion, feeling, purpose or interest among a group of people.\"\nIn this increasingly diverse society we live in, solidarity takes on a complex and multifaceted character as it forms bonds of interdependence among unique individuals living in a fast-changing environment and working within a complex division of labor.\nOne facet of solidarity fits well with the principles of communitarianism(社群主义). In this setting, individuals give up their own interests to serve the common good. However, its strict adherence to social cohesiveness and exclusiveness restricts expression and practice of individual differences and preferences. Consequently, individuality and autonomy are sacrificed to maintain the unity of the group.\nSolidarity is also viewed as a reaction to the extreme emphasis on individualism and freedom of choice. It goes against the liberal idea of unrestricted individuals who see society as a hindrance to their freedom to make choices and to act according to what is good for them regardless of how these will affect others.\nBut despite its many facets, social theorists have viewed solidarity as a key factor in bringing about order and progress in society. They believe that for human society to survive as it becomes more diverse, more new bonds need to be formed based on the differences. In the light of this, they regard a genuine solidarity as more than just shallow relationships based on common interests. Solidarity is not just a product of strategic calculations of how much it will benefit the self. It is not an exclusive class founded on the you-and-me-against-the-world principle. Rather, it is inclusive and is not forced. Acting out of solidarity means supporting and standing up for each other — in recognition that one's fate is in the fate of the other. It is a bond characterized by cooperative practices and strengthened by the concern for the well-being of others or the common good. It fights for the protection of human dignity and listens to and acts on the cries of the most vulnerable.", "47": "As interdependent beings we cannot thrive independent of others. We like to work with people who are like-minded, with whom we share a common interest, or who also believe in a cause we are serious about. This is evident in how we have organized ourselves into various collective groups such as nations, regions, communities, organizations, and families.\nDoes the group you belong to have a strong collective consciousness or shared beliefs? Is there interdependence among its members? Do you consider this group a significant part of your existence? If your answers are affirmative, then you are experiencing a micro version of solidarity.\nWhat is solidarity? Its simplest definition in the dictionary is: \"… a unifying opinion, feeling, purpose or interest among a group of people.\"\nIn this increasingly diverse society we live in, solidarity takes on a complex and multifaceted character as it forms bonds of interdependence among unique individuals living in a fast-changing environment and working within a complex division of labor.\nOne facet of solidarity fits well with the principles of communitarianism(社群主义). In this setting, individuals give up their own interests to serve the common good. However, its strict adherence to social cohesiveness and exclusiveness restricts expression and practice of individual differences and preferences. Consequently, individuality and autonomy are sacrificed to maintain the unity of the group.\nSolidarity is also viewed as a reaction to the extreme emphasis on individualism and freedom of choice. It goes against the liberal idea of unrestricted individuals who see society as a hindrance to their freedom to make choices and to act according to what is good for them regardless of how these will affect others.\nBut despite its many facets, social theorists have viewed solidarity as a key factor in bringing about order and progress in society. They believe that for human society to survive as it becomes more diverse, more new bonds need to be formed based on the differences. In the light of this, they regard a genuine solidarity as more than just shallow relationships based on common interests. Solidarity is not just a product of strategic calculations of how much it will benefit the self. It is not an exclusive class founded on the you-and-me-against-the-world principle. Rather, it is inclusive and is not forced. Acting out of solidarity means supporting and standing up for each other — in recognition that one's fate is in the fate of the other. It is a bond characterized by cooperative practices and strengthened by the concern for the well-being of others or the common good. It fights for the protection of human dignity and listens to and acts on the cries of the most vulnerable.", "48": "As interdependent beings we cannot thrive independent of others. We like to work with people who are like-minded, with whom we share a common interest, or who also believe in a cause we are serious about. This is evident in how we have organized ourselves into various collective groups such as nations, regions, communities, organizations, and families.\nDoes the group you belong to have a strong collective consciousness or shared beliefs? Is there interdependence among its members? Do you consider this group a significant part of your existence? If your answers are affirmative, then you are experiencing a micro version of solidarity.\nWhat is solidarity? Its simplest definition in the dictionary is: \"… a unifying opinion, feeling, purpose or interest among a group of people.\"\nIn this increasingly diverse society we live in, solidarity takes on a complex and multifaceted character as it forms bonds of interdependence among unique individuals living in a fast-changing environment and working within a complex division of labor.\nOne facet of solidarity fits well with the principles of communitarianism(社群主义). In this setting, individuals give up their own interests to serve the common good. However, its strict adherence to social cohesiveness and exclusiveness restricts expression and practice of individual differences and preferences. Consequently, individuality and autonomy are sacrificed to maintain the unity of the group.\nSolidarity is also viewed as a reaction to the extreme emphasis on individualism and freedom of choice. It goes against the liberal idea of unrestricted individuals who see society as a hindrance to their freedom to make choices and to act according to what is good for them regardless of how these will affect others.\nBut despite its many facets, social theorists have viewed solidarity as a key factor in bringing about order and progress in society. They believe that for human society to survive as it becomes more diverse, more new bonds need to be formed based on the differences. In the light of this, they regard a genuine solidarity as more than just shallow relationships based on common interests. Solidarity is not just a product of strategic calculations of how much it will benefit the self. It is not an exclusive class founded on the you-and-me-against-the-world principle. Rather, it is inclusive and is not forced. Acting out of solidarity means supporting and standing up for each other — in recognition that one's fate is in the fate of the other. It is a bond characterized by cooperative practices and strengthened by the concern for the well-being of others or the common good. It fights for the protection of human dignity and listens to and acts on the cries of the most vulnerable.", "49": "As interdependent beings we cannot thrive independent of others. We like to work with people who are like-minded, with whom we share a common interest, or who also believe in a cause we are serious about. This is evident in how we have organized ourselves into various collective groups such as nations, regions, communities, organizations, and families.\nDoes the group you belong to have a strong collective consciousness or shared beliefs? Is there interdependence among its members? Do you consider this group a significant part of your existence? If your answers are affirmative, then you are experiencing a micro version of solidarity.\nWhat is solidarity? Its simplest definition in the dictionary is: \"… a unifying opinion, feeling, purpose or interest among a group of people.\"\nIn this increasingly diverse society we live in, solidarity takes on a complex and multifaceted character as it forms bonds of interdependence among unique individuals living in a fast-changing environment and working within a complex division of labor.\nOne facet of solidarity fits well with the principles of communitarianism(社群主义). In this setting, individuals give up their own interests to serve the common good. However, its strict adherence to social cohesiveness and exclusiveness restricts expression and practice of individual differences and preferences. Consequently, individuality and autonomy are sacrificed to maintain the unity of the group.\nSolidarity is also viewed as a reaction to the extreme emphasis on individualism and freedom of choice. It goes against the liberal idea of unrestricted individuals who see society as a hindrance to their freedom to make choices and to act according to what is good for them regardless of how these will affect others.\nBut despite its many facets, social theorists have viewed solidarity as a key factor in bringing about order and progress in society. They believe that for human society to survive as it becomes more diverse, more new bonds need to be formed based on the differences. In the light of this, they regard a genuine solidarity as more than just shallow relationships based on common interests. Solidarity is not just a product of strategic calculations of how much it will benefit the self. It is not an exclusive class founded on the you-and-me-against-the-world principle. Rather, it is inclusive and is not forced. Acting out of solidarity means supporting and standing up for each other — in recognition that one's fate is in the fate of the other. It is a bond characterized by cooperative practices and strengthened by the concern for the well-being of others or the common good. It fights for the protection of human dignity and listens to and acts on the cries of the most vulnerable.", "50": "As interdependent beings we cannot thrive independent of others. We like to work with people who are like-minded, with whom we share a common interest, or who also believe in a cause we are serious about. This is evident in how we have organized ourselves into various collective groups such as nations, regions, communities, organizations, and families.\nDoes the group you belong to have a strong collective consciousness or shared beliefs? Is there interdependence among its members? Do you consider this group a significant part of your existence? If your answers are affirmative, then you are experiencing a micro version of solidarity.\nWhat is solidarity? Its simplest definition in the dictionary is: \"… a unifying opinion, feeling, purpose or interest among a group of people.\"\nIn this increasingly diverse society we live in, solidarity takes on a complex and multifaceted character as it forms bonds of interdependence among unique individuals living in a fast-changing environment and working within a complex division of labor.\nOne facet of solidarity fits well with the principles of communitarianism(社群主义). In this setting, individuals give up their own interests to serve the common good. However, its strict adherence to social cohesiveness and exclusiveness restricts expression and practice of individual differences and preferences. Consequently, individuality and autonomy are sacrificed to maintain the unity of the group.\nSolidarity is also viewed as a reaction to the extreme emphasis on individualism and freedom of choice. It goes against the liberal idea of unrestricted individuals who see society as a hindrance to their freedom to make choices and to act according to what is good for them regardless of how these will affect others.\nBut despite its many facets, social theorists have viewed solidarity as a key factor in bringing about order and progress in society. They believe that for human society to survive as it becomes more diverse, more new bonds need to be formed based on the differences. In the light of this, they regard a genuine solidarity as more than just shallow relationships based on common interests. Solidarity is not just a product of strategic calculations of how much it will benefit the self. It is not an exclusive class founded on the you-and-me-against-the-world principle. Rather, it is inclusive and is not forced. Acting out of solidarity means supporting and standing up for each other — in recognition that one's fate is in the fate of the other. It is a bond characterized by cooperative practices and strengthened by the concern for the well-being of others or the common good. It fights for the protection of human dignity and listens to and acts on the cries of the most vulnerable.", "51": "People who repeatedly give unwanted advice can be well-meaning and genuinely want to help. Others might do this out of a sense of self-worth around the ability to influence others. One study found that people with a high tendency to seek power were more likely to give advice than those with an opposite tendency.\nUnder the guise(外表) of unselfishness, people may be driven to give you unwanted advice because it makes them feel powerful or in control. They may not be fully aware of this drive, however.\nThese people may also display a problematic degree of emotional vulnerability, becoming upset very quickly, and taking a long time to calm down. It is possible that their emotions were only validated in childhood when they were at their loudest, encouraging them to adopt responses to discomfort that are excessive in most situations.\nWhen someone is giving advice in order to make themselves feel more powerful, there is underlying anxiety to their behavior that recipients of the advice tend to notice. It can be tempting in this situation to react harshly to the advice giver and to accuse them of being manipulative, but this approach might produce an undesired result.\nIf the act of giving advice is contributing actively to someone's feelings of self-worth, an outright(直率的) rejection may be perceived as a threat, activating their fight-or-flight response, possibly causing them to double down on their validation-seeking behavior, or leading to a larger conflict.\nThe key is to validate without over-identifying. You can let them know that you have heard them and appreciate where they are coming from without taking on the potentially damaging narrative that you could not have gotten by without their help. You had better say something like, \"Thanks for the idea. I have my own plan for handling this, but I really appreciate your perspective and will take it into consideration. Can I let you know when I need help in the future?\"\nIf you have trouble setting boundaries tactfully, prioritize working on your own ability to self-regulate. As uncomfortable as it may be to continuously receive, if you can respond with compassion, the situation will likely defuse much faster.\nThough it is usually not the intended outcome of giving unsolicited advice, many who receive it often feel stressed, offended, or simply annoyed by unwanted suggestions. Setting a boundary in this regard, if you feel you need one, is perfectly reasonable and something that can bring you increased emotional safety.\nIt is best to set boundaries in a way that takes into account the individual's underlying reasons for doing what they are doing to avoid unnecessary conflict and more stress.", "52": "People who repeatedly give unwanted advice can be well-meaning and genuinely want to help. Others might do this out of a sense of self-worth around the ability to influence others. One study found that people with a high tendency to seek power were more likely to give advice than those with an opposite tendency.\nUnder the guise(外表) of unselfishness, people may be driven to give you unwanted advice because it makes them feel powerful or in control. They may not be fully aware of this drive, however.\nThese people may also display a problematic degree of emotional vulnerability, becoming upset very quickly, and taking a long time to calm down. It is possible that their emotions were only validated in childhood when they were at their loudest, encouraging them to adopt responses to discomfort that are excessive in most situations.\nWhen someone is giving advice in order to make themselves feel more powerful, there is underlying anxiety to their behavior that recipients of the advice tend to notice. It can be tempting in this situation to react harshly to the advice giver and to accuse them of being manipulative, but this approach might produce an undesired result.\nIf the act of giving advice is contributing actively to someone's feelings of self-worth, an outright(直率的) rejection may be perceived as a threat, activating their fight-or-flight response, possibly causing them to double down on their validation-seeking behavior, or leading to a larger conflict.\nThe key is to validate without over-identifying. You can let them know that you have heard them and appreciate where they are coming from without taking on the potentially damaging narrative that you could not have gotten by without their help. You had better say something like, \"Thanks for the idea. I have my own plan for handling this, but I really appreciate your perspective and will take it into consideration. Can I let you know when I need help in the future?\"\nIf you have trouble setting boundaries tactfully, prioritize working on your own ability to self-regulate. As uncomfortable as it may be to continuously receive, if you can respond with compassion, the situation will likely defuse much faster.\nThough it is usually not the intended outcome of giving unsolicited advice, many who receive it often feel stressed, offended, or simply annoyed by unwanted suggestions. Setting a boundary in this regard, if you feel you need one, is perfectly reasonable and something that can bring you increased emotional safety.\nIt is best to set boundaries in a way that takes into account the individual's underlying reasons for doing what they are doing to avoid unnecessary conflict and more stress.", "53": "People who repeatedly give unwanted advice can be well-meaning and genuinely want to help. Others might do this out of a sense of self-worth around the ability to influence others. One study found that people with a high tendency to seek power were more likely to give advice than those with an opposite tendency.\nUnder the guise(外表) of unselfishness, people may be driven to give you unwanted advice because it makes them feel powerful or in control. They may not be fully aware of this drive, however.\nThese people may also display a problematic degree of emotional vulnerability, becoming upset very quickly, and taking a long time to calm down. It is possible that their emotions were only validated in childhood when they were at their loudest, encouraging them to adopt responses to discomfort that are excessive in most situations.\nWhen someone is giving advice in order to make themselves feel more powerful, there is underlying anxiety to their behavior that recipients of the advice tend to notice. It can be tempting in this situation to react harshly to the advice giver and to accuse them of being manipulative, but this approach might produce an undesired result.\nIf the act of giving advice is contributing actively to someone's feelings of self-worth, an outright(直率的) rejection may be perceived as a threat, activating their fight-or-flight response, possibly causing them to double down on their validation-seeking behavior, or leading to a larger conflict.\nThe key is to validate without over-identifying. You can let them know that you have heard them and appreciate where they are coming from without taking on the potentially damaging narrative that you could not have gotten by without their help. You had better say something like, \"Thanks for the idea. I have my own plan for handling this, but I really appreciate your perspective and will take it into consideration. Can I let you know when I need help in the future?\"\nIf you have trouble setting boundaries tactfully, prioritize working on your own ability to self-regulate. As uncomfortable as it may be to continuously receive, if you can respond with compassion, the situation will likely defuse much faster.\nThough it is usually not the intended outcome of giving unsolicited advice, many who receive it often feel stressed, offended, or simply annoyed by unwanted suggestions. Setting a boundary in this regard, if you feel you need one, is perfectly reasonable and something that can bring you increased emotional safety.\nIt is best to set boundaries in a way that takes into account the individual's underlying reasons for doing what they are doing to avoid unnecessary conflict and more stress.", "54": "People who repeatedly give unwanted advice can be well-meaning and genuinely want to help. Others might do this out of a sense of self-worth around the ability to influence others. One study found that people with a high tendency to seek power were more likely to give advice than those with an opposite tendency.\nUnder the guise(外表) of unselfishness, people may be driven to give you unwanted advice because it makes them feel powerful or in control. They may not be fully aware of this drive, however.\nThese people may also display a problematic degree of emotional vulnerability, becoming upset very quickly, and taking a long time to calm down. It is possible that their emotions were only validated in childhood when they were at their loudest, encouraging them to adopt responses to discomfort that are excessive in most situations.\nWhen someone is giving advice in order to make themselves feel more powerful, there is underlying anxiety to their behavior that recipients of the advice tend to notice. It can be tempting in this situation to react harshly to the advice giver and to accuse them of being manipulative, but this approach might produce an undesired result.\nIf the act of giving advice is contributing actively to someone's feelings of self-worth, an outright(直率的) rejection may be perceived as a threat, activating their fight-or-flight response, possibly causing them to double down on their validation-seeking behavior, or leading to a larger conflict.\nThe key is to validate without over-identifying. You can let them know that you have heard them and appreciate where they are coming from without taking on the potentially damaging narrative that you could not have gotten by without their help. You had better say something like, \"Thanks for the idea. I have my own plan for handling this, but I really appreciate your perspective and will take it into consideration. Can I let you know when I need help in the future?\"\nIf you have trouble setting boundaries tactfully, prioritize working on your own ability to self-regulate. As uncomfortable as it may be to continuously receive, if you can respond with compassion, the situation will likely defuse much faster.\nThough it is usually not the intended outcome of giving unsolicited advice, many who receive it often feel stressed, offended, or simply annoyed by unwanted suggestions. Setting a boundary in this regard, if you feel you need one, is perfectly reasonable and something that can bring you increased emotional safety.\nIt is best to set boundaries in a way that takes into account the individual's underlying reasons for doing what they are doing to avoid unnecessary conflict and more stress.", "55": "People who repeatedly give unwanted advice can be well-meaning and genuinely want to help. Others might do this out of a sense of self-worth around the ability to influence others. One study found that people with a high tendency to seek power were more likely to give advice than those with an opposite tendency.\nUnder the guise(外表) of unselfishness, people may be driven to give you unwanted advice because it makes them feel powerful or in control. They may not be fully aware of this drive, however.\nThese people may also display a problematic degree of emotional vulnerability, becoming upset very quickly, and taking a long time to calm down. It is possible that their emotions were only validated in childhood when they were at their loudest, encouraging them to adopt responses to discomfort that are excessive in most situations.\nWhen someone is giving advice in order to make themselves feel more powerful, there is underlying anxiety to their behavior that recipients of the advice tend to notice. It can be tempting in this situation to react harshly to the advice giver and to accuse them of being manipulative, but this approach might produce an undesired result.\nIf the act of giving advice is contributing actively to someone's feelings of self-worth, an outright(直率的) rejection may be perceived as a threat, activating their fight-or-flight response, possibly causing them to double down on their validation-seeking behavior, or leading to a larger conflict.\nThe key is to validate without over-identifying. You can let them know that you have heard them and appreciate where they are coming from without taking on the potentially damaging narrative that you could not have gotten by without their help. You had better say something like, \"Thanks for the idea. I have my own plan for handling this, but I really appreciate your perspective and will take it into consideration. Can I let you know when I need help in the future?\"\nIf you have trouble setting boundaries tactfully, prioritize working on your own ability to self-regulate. As uncomfortable as it may be to continuously receive, if you can respond with compassion, the situation will likely defuse much faster.\nThough it is usually not the intended outcome of giving unsolicited advice, many who receive it often feel stressed, offended, or simply annoyed by unwanted suggestions. Setting a boundary in this regard, if you feel you need one, is perfectly reasonable and something that can bring you increased emotional safety.\nIt is best to set boundaries in a way that takes into account the individual's underlying reasons for doing what they are doing to avoid unnecessary conflict and more stress."}, "questions": [{"id": "2025-12-2-l-1", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 1, "stem": "What does the woman say about the food at the canteen?", "options": ["A) It is absurdly expensive.", "B) It is appropriately priced.", "C) It brings shame on the university.", "D) It caters to students and teachers."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （态度观点题） It is appropriately priced.\n【定位】W: I don't, actually. I personally think they're perfectly appropriate.\n女：其实我不同意。我个人觉得它们（价格）完全合理。\n【信号】被问者的直接应答 + 转折否定。男士先抛出 the prices are absurd 并反问 Don't you\nagree，女士的应答 I don't, actually 是一句明确的否定回应，紧接 I personally think\nthey're perfectly appropriate——『被问者的直接否定+亮明己见』正是答案句信号，女\n士的真实态度全在这句。\n【替换】perfectly appropriate ↔ appropriately priced\n同根词改写：appropriate→appropriately priced\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nIt is absurdly expensive. 直接挪用了录音里的 absurd，但说 the prices are absurd 的是\n男士、不是女士，女士恰恰否定了这一点。听到 absurd 就选 A，正中『听到原词就选』的\n陷阱。\n借⽤原⽂：the prices are absurd\nC) 原词陷阱\nIt brings shame on the university. 借用了录音里的 shame，但男士那句 Shame about\nthe food. 是英式口语『可惜了这食物』的意思，并非『给学校丢脸』，且也不是女士对食\n物的评价。听到 shame 就联想到『丢脸』属于偷换概念。\n借⽤原⽂：Shame about the food.\nD) 答非所问\nIt caters to students and teachers. 挪用了录音 both for students and teachers，但那\n是男士在说食堂作为聚会点适合师生，谈的是场所、不是『食物』，与题目问的食物评价不\n沾边。\n借⽤原⽂：both for students and teachers", "points": ["听力·长对话", "态度观点题"]}, {"id": "2025-12-2-l-2", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 2, "stem": "How does the woman feel about the canteen's sweet things or fancy delicacies?", "options": ["A) They never really appeal to her.", "B) They are never actually good value.", "C) They are within students' budgets.", "D) They always come at a high speed."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （态度观点题） They never really appeal to her.\n【定位】I never really crave sweet things or any fancy delicacies.\n我从来都不怎么想吃甜食或者什么精致美味。\n【信号】含蓄否定 never...crave 表态度。女士说 I never really crave sweet things or any fancy\ndelicacies，crave（渴望）配 never 是一句明确的『提不起兴趣』表态，正是答案信号\n——态度题就听这种带 never/really 的情感动词。\n【替换】I never really crave sweet things or any fancy delicacies ↔ They never really appeal to her.\n主被动视角转换：我不想要它→它不吸引我\n【排除】B) 答非所问\nThey are never actually good value. 蹭了录音里关于价格的讨论（desserts are\noverpriced），但题目问的是女士对甜点的『感受/兴趣』，女士给出的态度是『不想\n吃』，而非评价它们『不划算』。把态度偷换成性价比。\n借⽤原⽂：desserts are overpriced\nC) 与原文相反\nThey are within students' budgets. 挪用了前文男士谈预算的 beyond most students'\nbudgets，且与原文相反（男士说甜点超出预算），更何况它讲的是价格不是女士的态度，\n双重错位。\n借⽤原⽂：beyond most students' budgets\nD) 张冠李戴\nThey always come at a high speed. 借用了录音里关于上菜快的描述（They also come\nout really fast），但那是男士夸炒蛋出餐快，被安到了甜点和女士态度上，张冠李戴又答\n非所问。\n借⽤原⽂：They also come out really fast", "points": ["听力·长对话", "态度观点题"]}, {"id": "2025-12-2-l-3", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 3, "stem": "What does the man say is one of the best things about the canteen?", "options": ["A) The food.", "B) The space.", "C) The speed.", "D) The environment."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） The speed.\n【定位】Actually, that's one of the best things about the canteen — the speed.\n其实，那是这个食堂最棒的一点之一——速度。\n【信号】最高级 one of the best 后直接点名。男士说 that's one of the best things about the\ncanteen — the speed，破折号后直接点出 the speed，是最高级语气+明确点名的答案信\n号，落点一目了然。\n【替换】the speed ↔ The speed.\n原词直接对应\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nThe food. 是全程反复出现的话题词，但男士对食物的评价是 the food is just OK，远谈不\n上『最好之一』，听到 food 高频出现就选属于被话题词诱导。\n借⽤原⽂：the food is just OK\nB) 答非所问\nThe space. 对应录音后文 It's a very versatile space，那是夸空间多用途，但并非男士点\n名的『best things』之一，落点不符。\n借⽤原⽂：It's a very versatile space\nD) 张冠李戴\nThe environment. 对应女士那句 it's such a great environment too，确实被夸，但夸环\n境的是女士、且不是男士点名的『最好之一』，张冠李戴又非答案落点。\n借⽤原⽂：it's such a great environment too", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-12-2-l-4", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 4, "stem": "What did the canteen undergo a few years ago?", "options": ["A) A complete refurnishing.", "B) A ruinous burning down.", "C) A substantial expansion.", "D) A massive renovation."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） A massive renovation.\n【定位】Apparently, they did a massive renovation a few years ago.\n据说他们几年前做过一次大规模的翻新。\n【信号】时间状语 a few years ago 标记答案句。女士说 they did a massive renovation a few\nyears ago，时间状语 a few years ago 与题干完全对上，是答案句的定位信号；其谓语\ndid a massive renovation 即所问事件。\n【替换】a massive renovation ↔ A massive renovation.\n原词对应，但需与近义项 expansion 切开\n【排除】A) 偷换概念\nA complete refurnishing. 蹭了改造话题，但 refurnishing 是『重新配置家具』，与录音 a\nmassive renovation（整体翻新）程度和性质都不同，是偷换概念。\n借⽤原⽂：a massive renovation\nB) 未提及\nA ruinous burning down. 是『毁灭性烧毁』，录音通篇在夸食堂漂亮、做过翻新，根本没\n提任何火灾，纯属无中生有。\nC) 偷换概念\nA substantial expansion. 与答案 D 同样表『大规模』，借 massive/substantial 的近似制\n造混淆，但 expansion 是『扩建、变大』，录音说的是 renovation（翻新），并非扩大面\n积，差在动作名词上。\n借⽤原⽂：a massive renovation", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-12-2-l-5", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 5, "stem": "According to the woman, what view about today's young people is not really justified?", "options": ["A) They have a poor work ethic.", "B) They don't get along with managers.", "C) They are struggling in the workplace.", "D) They aren't as varied as their predecessors."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） They have a poor work ethic.\n【定位】We often hear young people today have a poor work ethic. That's not really justified though,\nis it?\n我们常听说如今的年轻人职业道德差。不过这种说法其实站不住脚，对吧？\n【信号】女方自问句 + that's not justified。答案句出自女方之口：先抛出常见说法 young\npeople today have a poor work ethic，紧接 That's not really justified though 直接呼\n应题干的 not justified。「常听说……不过站不住脚」这个先立后破的句式，就是答案落点\n的信号。\n【替换】young people today have a poor work ethic ↔ They have a poor work ethic.\n几乎原句搬到选项\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\n选项 They don't get along with managers. 盗用了对话主题里反复出现的 managers，但\n女方在 P1 里判为「站不住脚」的说法是职业道德差，从没说年轻人和管理者合不来，属答\n非所问。\n借⽤原⽂：young people today have a poor work ethic\nC) 张冠李戴\n选项 They are struggling in the workplace. 借了后文 if you are struggling to work\nwith young people 里的 struggling，但那是说「管理者难以与年轻人共事」，并非女方\n在第 1 段判为不成立的看法，张冠李戴。\n借⽤原⽂：if you are struggling to work with young people\nD) 张冠李戴\n选项 They aren't as varied as their predecessors. 与男方 young people of this age\ngroup are as varied and multifaceted as any other generation 正好相反：男方说「和\n任何一代一样多样」，选项却说「不如前人多样」，极性被反转，且这话也不是女方所说。\n借⽤原⽂：as varied and multifaceted as any other generation", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-12-2-l-6", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 6, "stem": "What does the man say is important for managers to get along better with young workers?", "options": ["A) Learning about the way they adhere to their own values.", "B) Recognizing how they try to strike a work-life balance.", "C) Knowing how they struggle to connect with managers.", "D) Understanding the way they seek career advancement."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） Recognizing how they try to strike a work-life balance.\n【定位】Well, it's essential to connect with them to learn about their values. It's also really important\nto recognize the way young people want to balance their work life with their personal life.\n嗯，与他们建立联系、了解他们的价值观是必不可少的。同样很重要的是，认识到年轻人想\n要平衡工作与个人生活的方式。\n【信号】两个强调句 essential / important。男方连用 it's essential to 和 It's also really\nimportant to recognize 两处强调，正面回答「什么重要」。题干落在 work-life balance\n一支，对应的正是 recognize the way young people want to balance their work life\nwith their personal life 这句。\n【替换】recognize the way young people want to balance their work life with their personal life ↔\nRecognizing how they try to strike a work-life balance.\nbalance their work...life=work-life balance\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n选项 Learning about the way they adhere to their own values. 盗用了 learn about\ntheir values 里的 values，可男方说的是「了解他们的价值观」，不是「了解他们如何坚守\n自己的价值观（adhere to）」，多塞了 adhere 这层原文没有的意思，答非所问。\n借⽤原⽂：connect with them to learn about their values\nC) 原词陷阱\n选项 Knowing how they struggle to connect with managers. 把 connect with them 的\nconnect 与别处的 struggle、主题词 managers 拼到一起，但男方说的是「与他们建立联\n系去了解价值观」，从没说年轻人「难以与管理者建立联系」，纯属拼凑。\n借⽤原⽂：it's essential to connect with them\nD) 原词陷阱\n选项 Understanding the way they seek career advancement. 借了 defer happiness in\nreturn for career advancement 的 career advancement，但那出自女方提问、且说的是\n年轻人「不愿」为升迁牺牲幸福；男方此处答「重要的是」并未把「理解他们如何追求升\n迁」列为关键，张冠李戴又答非所问。\n借⽤原⽂：defer happiness in return for career advancement", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-12-2-l-7", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 7, "stem": "How do young people measure their productivity levels according to the man?", "options": ["A) In terms of the number of hours spent.", "B) By the amount of work they perform.", "C) In terms of the work done in 8 hours.", "D) By the quality of the work they do."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） By the quality of the work they do.\n【定位】Generally, young people believe their productivity should not be measured by the number of\nhours they work, since someone can work for eight hours and accomplish next to nothing of\nsignificance for the company. Instead, young people measure their productivity levels by the\nquality of the work they perform.\n一般来说，年轻人认为生产力不应以工作的小时数来衡量，因为有人可能干了八小时却几乎\n没为公司做出任何有意义的成果。相反，年轻人是以他们所做工作的质量来衡量生产力的。\n【信号】not…by hours, Instead…by quality。男方先用 should not be measured by the\nnumber of hours 否定按时长，再用 Instead 引出真正口径 by the quality of the work\nthey perform。Instead 之后才是答案落点，前半是被否定的干扰。\n【替换】by the quality of the work they perform ↔ By the quality of the work they do.\nperform=do，其余原样\n【排除】A) 与原文相反\n选项 In terms of the number of hours spent. 几乎照搬 the number of hours they\nwork，但这恰恰是男方用 should not be measured 明确否定掉的口径，选它正中陷阱。\n借⽤原⽂：should not be measured by the number of hours they work\nB) 偷换概念\n选项 By the amount of work they perform. 借了原文的 work、perform，但把口径换成\n「工作的数量（amount）」，男方说的是质量而非数量，偷换了概念。\n借⽤原⽂：the quality of the work they perform\nC) 原词陷阱\n选项 In terms of the work done in 8 hours. 盗用 work for eight hours 的 eight\nhours，但那只是男方举例说明「干八小时也可能毫无成果」、用来反证不该按时长，并非\n衡量口径本身，答非所问。\n借⽤原⽂：someone can work for eight hours and accomplish next to nothing", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-12-2-l-8", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 8, "stem": "What companies does the man think would appeal to young workers?", "options": ["A) Those whose culture facilitates their advancement.", "B) Those allowing them to do projects of their choice.", "C) Those whose values align with their own.", "D) Those refraining from discrediting their perspectives."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） Those whose values align with their own.\n【定位】Young workers want to work on projects that matter to themselves, the company or the world\nat large. They must feel the need to stay working at a company whose values align with\ntheir own.\n年轻员工想做对自己、对公司或对整个世界都有意义的项目。他们必然会想留在一家价值观\n与自己一致的公司。\n【信号】末段总结句 a company whose values align。对话收尾男方给出结论 stay working at a\ncompany whose values align with their own，直接定义了吸引年轻人的公司类型。这是\n对话末尾的总结落点，正面回答题干。\n【替换】a company whose values align with their own ↔ Those whose values align with their own.\na company=Those，定语原样\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n选项 Those whose culture facilitates their advancement. 把主题氛围词 culture 与女方\n提问里的 career advancement 拼合，但男方末段说的是「价值观一致」，从没说公司文化\n要「助推升迁」，纯属拼凑且不是结论。\n借⽤原⽂：defer happiness in return for career advancement\nB) 偷换概念\n选项 Those allowing them to do projects of their choice. 盗用 work on projects that\nmatter 的 projects，可男方说的是做「有意义的（matter）」项目，不是「他们自选的\n（of their choice）」项目，偷偷改了限定，与原文不符。\n借⽤原⽂：work on projects that matter to themselves, the company or the world at large\nD) 原词陷阱\n选项 Those refraining from discrediting their perspectives. 借了 Such a perspective\ncan be hard to discredit 的 discredit、perspective，但那是女方提问里说「这种看法很\n难被否定」，并非男方对「吸引人的公司」给出的特征，张冠李戴。\n借⽤原⽂：Such a perspective can be hard to discredit, can't it?", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-12-2-l-9", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 9, "stem": "What does a new study suggest about restaurants including vegetarian sections on their menus?", "options": ["A) They may unconsciously help customers choose greener dishes.", "B) They may unintentionally encourage customers to eat meat.", "C) They raise customers' environmental awareness.", "D) They enable customers to eat in a healthy way."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） They may unintentionally encourage customers to eat meat.\n【定位】Although it sounds counter-intuitive, restaurants which include specific vegetarian\nsections on their menus may be unintentionally encouraging customers to eat meat, a new\nstudy suggests.\n尽管听起来有违直觉，但一项新研究表明，在菜单上专门设素食专区的餐厅，可能正无意中\n鼓励顾客去吃肉。\n【信号】让步转折Although后是真意。句首 Although it sounds counter-intuitive（尽管听起来违\n反直觉）是让步信号，真正的研究结论在它之后——may be unintentionally\nencouraging customers to eat meat，加上 a new study suggests 与题干 suggest 对\n应，这就是答案句。\n【替换】unintentionally encouraging customers to eat meat ↔\nunintentionally encourage customers to eat meat\n近乎同形的直接对应\n【排除】A) 与原文相反\nA说\"无意中帮顾客选更环保的菜\"，盗用了开头 moving away from environmentally\nunfriendly meat towards greener dishes 里的 greener，但那是\"餐厅想达到的初衷\"，研\n究的发现恰恰相反——是鼓励吃肉，A把结果方向说反了。\n借⽤原⽂：moving away from environmentally unfriendly meat towards greener dishes\nC) 未提及\nC说\"提高顾客的环保意识\"，听上去与开头 eat more sustainably 沾边，但录音从没说研究\n发现能\"提升意识\"，只谈\"实际点菜选择\"，属于顺着环保话题硬编出来的结论。\nD) 未提及\nD说\"让顾客以健康方式进食\"，但全篇围绕\"环保/吃肉还是吃素\"，从未谈\"健康\"这个角度，\n属于另起话题的无中生有。", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-12-2-l-10", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 10, "stem": "How was the study carried out on 750 participants?", "options": ["A) By inviting them to taste different kinds of food.", "B) By observing the eating behavior of strict vegetarians.", "C) By comparing participants eating vegetarian food frequently with those doing so rarely.", "D) By requiring participants to choose a meal as if they were feasting at a family gathering."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\nBy comparing participants eating vegetarian food frequently with those doing so rarely.\n【定位】The study involved 750 people, half of whom frequently ate vegetarian food and half of\nwhom rarely ate it. No vegetarians were included.\n研究共有750人参与，其中一半人经常吃素食，另一半人很少吃素食。没有素食者被纳入\n研究。\n【信号】数字750与分组描述句。听到题干里的数字 750 出现在 The study involved 750 people，\n紧跟其后的 half of whom frequently ate vegetarian food and half of whom rarely ate\nit 正是研究设计的核心——把人分成\"常吃素\"和\"很少吃素\"两组对比，这就是答案句。\n【替换】half of whom frequently ate vegetarian food and half of whom rarely ate it ↔\ncomparing participants eating vegetarian food frequently with those doing so rarely\n对照设计的同义改写\n【排除】A) 偷换概念\nA说\"请他们品尝不同食物\"，但录音只让受试者\"看菜单选一餐\"given different menus and\nasked to choose a meal，并没有真去吃/尝，属把\"选菜单\"偷换成\"尝食物\"。\n借⽤原⽂：given different menus and asked to choose a meal\nB) 与原文相反\nB说\"观察严格素食者的进食行为\"，盗用了原文反复出现的 vegetarian，但录音明说 No\nvegetarians were included——根本没有素食者参加，更没有\"观察其行为\"，与原文相\n反。\n借⽤原⽂：No vegetarians were included\nD) 原词陷阱\nD说\"要求受试者像在家庭聚会上盛宴那样选餐\"，盗用了 asked to choose a meal，但原文\n的设定是 as if they were eating out with friends（像和朋友外出就餐），不是\"家庭聚会\n盛宴\"，场景被偷换。\n借⽤原⽂：asked to choose a meal as if they were eating out with friends", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-12-2-l-11", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 11, "stem": "How do altered menu designs affect frequent eaters of vegetarian food?", "options": ["A) They are less likely to choose a vegetarian dish.", "B) They find vegetarian dishes become more appealing.", "C) They tend to consume larger portions of unhealthy food.", "D) They are less likely to choose dishes recommended by chefs."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） They are less likely to choose a vegetarian dish.\n【定位】But again, these altered menu designs backfired with those who ate vegetarian food more\nfrequently, leading them to be less likely to choose a vegetarian dish.\n但同样地，这些改动过的菜单设计对那些更常吃素食的人产生了反效果，使他们更不可能去\n选一道素菜。\n【信号】But again转折指向常吃者。But again 是转折信号，前一句讲改菜单让\"少吃素的人\"更愿\n点素菜，转折之后才轮到题目问的\"常吃素的人\"——backfired with those who ate\nvegetarian food more frequently，结果是 less likely to choose a vegetarian dish，这\n就是答案句。\n【替换】less likely to choose a vegetarian dish ↔ less likely to choose a vegetarian dish\n几乎逐字一致\n【排除】B) 张冠李戴\nB说\"他们觉得素菜更有吸引力\"，盗用了原文 a more appealing description of a non-\nmeat meal 里的 appealing，但\"更想点素菜\"这个正向结果发生在\"少吃素的人\"身上，题目\n问的\"常吃素的人\"恰恰相反，张冠李戴。\n借⽤原⽂：a more appealing description of a non-meat meal\nC) 未提及\nC说\"他们会吃更多不健康食物\"，但录音只谈\"是否点素菜\"，从没提\"吃更大份的不健康食\n物\"，属凭印象编出来的结果。\nD) 原词陷阱\nD说\"他们更不可能点主厨推荐的菜\"，盗用了原文 presenting a vegetarian dish as the\nchef's recommendation 里的 chef，但原文说的结果是\"更不可能点素菜\"，而非\"不点主厨\n推荐的菜\"，把对象从\"素菜\"偷换成\"主厨推荐菜\"。\n借⽤原⽂：presenting a vegetarian dish as the chef's recommendation", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-12-2-l-12", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 12, "stem": "What does the passage say about Nottingham Trent University and the Chinese Academy of Sciences?", "options": ["A) They have found a solution to the global problem of air pollution.", "B) They have managed to freeze polluting particles to around –18 °C.", "C) They have been seeking a way to prevent pollutants from penetrating indoors.", "D) They have been cooperating closely on developing a new type of air-conditioner."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） They have been seeking a way to prevent pollutants from penetrating indoors.\n【定位】Nottingham Trent University and the Chinese Academy of Sciences have been trying to find a\nway to prevent deadly outdoor air pollution from penetrating indoors.\n诺丁汉特伦特大学与中国科学院一直在设法找到一种途径，阻止致命的室外空气污染渗入\n室内。\n【信号】段首总述句。录音开篇第一句就以两家机构为主语作总述：have been trying to find a\nway to prevent deadly outdoor air pollution from penetrating indoors，篇章独白的研\n究目的常在段首点明，这句正是答案句。\n【替换】have been trying to find a way ↔ have been seeking a way\ntrying to find = seeking\nprevent deadly outdoor air pollution from penetrating indoors ↔\nprevent pollutants from penetrating indoors\n几乎原句保留\n【排除】A) 与原文相反\nA 项盗用了第二段的 global problem 一词，说他们已 found a solution to the global\nproblem，但录音用的是 trying to find a way（还在尝试），并未说已彻底解决全球性难\n题，把探索说成了已完成，程度过满。\n借⽤原⽂：Outdoor air pollution in cities is a global problem\nB) 原词陷阱\nB 项盗用录音第二句的原词 frozen to around –18，但 –18 ℃ 冷冻只是实现目标的手段细\n节，题目问的是这两家机构在做什么（目的），用手段冒充目的，答非所问。\n借⽤原⽂：if pollution is frozen to around –18 ℃ in a condensing tube\nD) 偷换概念\nD 项说两家在合作开发一种新型空调（developing a new type of air-conditioner）；录\n音只说有望对现有空调做 simple modification of air conditioning units（简单改造），\n从未说开发新空调，是无中生有式偷换。\n借⽤原⽂：simple modification of air conditioning units so that they can also clean polluted\nindoor air", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-12-2-l-13", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 13, "stem": "What do we learn from the passage about existing technologies to purify indoor air?", "options": ["A) They can be costly and inefficient.", "B) They are constantly being perfected.", "C) They are by-products of health research.", "D) They can save over 40,000 lives each year."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） They can be costly and inefficient.\n【定位】While there are some existing technologies to purify indoor air, they can be inefficient,\nexpensive or produce harmful by-products.\n虽然已有一些净化室内空气的现有技术，但它们可能低效、昂贵，或产生有害的副产品。\n【信号】While 让步后转折出真意。句子以 While there are some existing technologies 让步开\n头，承认有现有技术，紧接着的 they can be inefficient, expensive 才是真正要说的评\n价；让步与转折之后的部分是答案落点。\n【替换】expensive ↔ costly\nexpensive = costly\ninefficient ↔ inefficient\n低效原词对应\n【排除】B) 与原文相反\nB 项说现有技术 constantly being perfected（不断完善），是正面评价；但录音对它的评\n价是 inefficient, expensive or produce harmful by-products（负面），极性正好弄反。\n借⽤原⽂：they can be inefficient, expensive or produce harmful by-products\nC) 偷换概念\nC 项盗用录音原词 by-products，但录音说的是现有技术可能 produce harmful by-\nproducts（产生有害副产品），C 却说它们本身是健康研究的副产品（by-products of\nhealth research），张冠李戴改了主体关系。\n借⽤原⽂：produce harmful by-products\nD) 原词陷阱\nD 项盗用录音的醒目数字 40,000，说现有技术每年能救四万多人；但录音里 40,000 讲的是\nover 40,000 early deaths in Britain each year（每年四万多人过早死亡），是死亡数而非\n现有技术挽救的人数，数字对象被错配。\n借⽤原⽂：pollution is linked to over 40,000 early deaths in Britain each year", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-12-2-l-14", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 14, "stem": "What do people tend to do when outdoor air quality is poor?", "options": ["A) They keep their windows closed.", "B) They spend a lot more time indoors.", "C) They make more use of air-conditioners.", "D) They try various ways to purify indoor air."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） They spend a lot more time indoors.\n【定位】When outdoor air quality is poor, people tend to spend even more time indoors.\n当户外空气质量差时，人们往往会在室内待更长时间。\n【信号】题干原词 tend to 直接对应。录音里 When outdoor air quality is poor, people tend to\nspend even more time indoors 与题干用词几乎一字不差，tend to 这一标志词直接锁定\n答案句，属顺序定位。\n【替换】spend even more time indoors ↔ spend a lot more time indoors\neven more = a lot more\n【排除】A) 未提及\nA 项说人们关窗（keep their windows closed），听起来很合常识，但录音从头到尾没提\n到关窗这个动作，属凭常识脑补、原文未提及。\nC) 未提及\nC 项说人们更多用空调（make more use of air-conditioners），录音里 air conditioning\n是讲技术改造方向，并未说人们在空气差时更多使用空调，属常识脑补、原文未提及人们这\n一行为。\nD) 张冠李戴\nD 项盗用话题原词 purify indoor air，但录音里净化室内空气是技术/研究要做的事，不是\n人们在空气差时采取的做法，主体被张冠李戴。\n借⽤原⽂：While there are some existing technologies to purify indoor air", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-12-2-l-15", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 15, "stem": "What did Professor Gang Pan of the Chinese Academy of Sciences say about their study?", "options": ["A) It shows the importance of eliminating indoor pollution.", "B) It may change the way of future environmental research.", "C) It demonstrates the fruitful cooperation between UK and Chinese scientists.", "D) It may make household appliances serve an additional air cleaner function."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） It may make household appliances serve an additional air cleaner function.\n【定位】Our study makes it possible to add an air cleaner option to household appliances in areas\nwhich might experience extremely poor air conditions.\n我们的研究使得在空气质量可能极差的地区，给家用电器增加一项空气净化功能成为可能。\n【信号】专家引语中的用途句。在专家 Professor Gang Pan 的引语里，Our study makes it\npossible to add an air cleaner option to household appliances 直接点出研究的实际用\n途，是被引用原话中评价研究意义的关键句，即答案句。\n【替换】add an air cleaner option to household appliances ↔\nmake household appliances serve an additional air cleaner function\nadd option = additional function\n【排除】A) 过度推断\nA 项说研究显示了消除室内污染的重要性（importance of eliminating indoor\npollution）；录音里专家说的是 By controlling indoor air pollution（控制污染）并讲其\n用途，并未把重点放在论证消除污染有多重要上，A 拔高且偏离原话。\n借⽤原⽂：By controlling indoor air pollution and improving air quality in this way\nB) 过度推断\nB 项说研究可能改变未来环境研究的方式（change the way of future environmental\nresearch），录音里专家只谈本研究的用途与对公共健康的好处，从未说会改变未来研究的\n方式，属无中生有的拔高。\nC) 原词陷阱\nC 项说研究证明了中英科学家的成功合作（cooperation between UK and Chinese\nscientists），录音开头确有两家机构合作的事实 Nottingham Trent University and the\nChinese Academy of Sciences，但题目问专家对研究本身说了什么，专家原话谈的是用途\n而非合作，借合作事实答非所问。\n借⽤原⽂：Nottingham Trent University and the Chinese Academy of Sciences have been trying to\nfind a way", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-12-2-l-16", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 16, "stem": "What do we learn about the well-known mirror test?", "options": ["A) It distinguishes different species of animals.", "B) It reflects distinct traits in animal behavior.", "C) It demonstrates animals' ability to recognize themselves.", "D) It gauges the level of intelligence of various animal species."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） It demonstrates animals' ability to recognize themselves.\n【定位】This is a test that demonstrates self-recognition in animals. Only a few animals, such as\nhumans, apes, dolphins and some birds, can recognize themselves in a mirror.\n这是一项检验动物自我识别能力的测试。只有少数动物，例如人类、猿、海豚和某些鸟类，\n能在镜子里认出自己。\n【信号】下定义句 This is a test that…。录音用 This is a test that demonstrates self-\nrecognition in animals 这样的\"This is a test that…\"下定义句式，直接点出测试的功能，\n这种\"给某物下定义\"的句子就是细节题的答案句信号。\n【替换】demonstrates self-recognition in animals ↔\ndemonstrates animals' ability to recognize themselves\n名词↔动词的同义改写\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n录音里确实念到 A certain species of fish 的 species（物种），选项 distinguishes\ndifferent species of animals 就盗用这个词造\"分辨物种\"，但测试是测\"自我识别\"，不是分\n辨物种，听到 species 就选会中招。\n借⽤原⽂：A certain species of fish\nB) 偷换概念\n录音通篇讲 behavior、behavioral，选项 reflects distinct traits in animal behavior 盗用\nbehavior 造\"反映行为的不同特征\"，但测试反映的是\"能否自我识别\"，不是\"区分行为差\n异\"，概念被悄悄换掉。\n借⽤原⽂：This ability suggests a level of higher intelligence and awareness\nD) 过度推断\n录音说 This ability suggests a level of higher intelligence，选项 gauges the level of\nintelligence of various animal species 盗用 intelligence 和 level 造\"测量各物种智力等\n级\"，但原文是\"自我识别这种能力暗示了较高智力\"，测试本身测的是自我识别，不是给智力\n分级。\n借⽤原⽂：This ability suggests a level of higher intelligence and awareness", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-12-2-l-17", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 17, "stem": "What does the study by the speaker's team show about the fish they experimented with?", "options": ["A) It meets few of the behavioral criteria set in the mirror test.", "B) It behaves similarly to animals capable of self-recognition.", "C) It removes marks from itself to show self-awareness.", "D) It matches children over 18 months in intelligence."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） It behaves similarly to animals capable of self-recognition.\n【定位】The behaviors we observe leave little doubt that this fish behaviorally fulfills all criteria of\nthe mirror test as originally laid out.\n我们观察到的行为几乎可以确定，这条鱼在行为上满足了最初设定的镜子测试的全部标准。\n【信号】结果断言 leave little doubt that…。录音用 leave little doubt that this fish\nbehaviorally fulfills all criteria\"几乎无疑地满足全部标准\"这种强结果断言，是实验给出的\n核心结论，正是答案句信号。\n【替换】fulfills all criteria of the mirror test ↔ behaves similarly to animals capable of self-recognition\n满足标准=行为同已通过者\n【排除】A) 与原文相反\n录音念到 criteria，但说的是 fulfills all criteria（满足全部标准），选项 meets few of the\nbehavioral criteria 盗用 criteria 却改成\"几乎不满足\"，把肯定听成了否定，正好反了。\n借⽤原⽂：fulfills all criteria of the mirror test\nC) 偷换概念\n录音确有 scrape up against hard surfaces to remove marks\"蹭掉身上标记\"，选项\nremoves marks from itself to show self-awareness 盗用 remove marks，但把它直接等\n同于\"展示自我意识\"——而录音第三段明说\"是否算自我意识尚不清楚\"，这是把现象当定\n论，偷换了概念。\n借⽤原⽂：scrape up against hard surfaces to remove marks placed on its body\nD) 张冠李戴\n录音 This is not shown by children until around 18 months 里的 18 个月是说\"人类儿童\n到约18个月才有此能力\"，选项 matches children over 18 months in intelligence 盗用这\n个数字，硬说\"鱼的智力等同18个月以上的儿童\"，把讲儿童的话安到鱼头上。\n借⽤原⽂：This is not shown by children until around 18 months", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-12-2-l-18", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 18, "stem": "What does the speaker say about the results of their research?", "options": ["A) They have conclusively established the criteria of animal intelligence.", "B) They have advanced the concept of self-awareness to a higher level.", "C) They incorporate more perspectives than one.", "D) They raise more questions than answers."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） They raise more questions than answers.\n【定位】Results from our research raise more questions than answers.\n我们研究得出的结果，带来的问题比答案还多。\n【信号】结尾定调句 Results… raise…。末段以 Results from our research raise more questions\nthan answers 开篇，是讲者对整项研究结果的收束定调，这种\"结尾一句话总评结果\"正是\n答案句信号。\n【替换】raise more questions than answers ↔ They raise more questions than answers.\n近乎原句对应\n【排除】A) 与原文相反\n录音多次提 criteria，选项 conclusively established the criteria of animal intelligence\n盗用并说\"已确凿确立动物智力标准\"，但末段恰恰说\"问题更多\"、还呼吁 stop looking at\nresponses to the mirror as a decisive test，根本没\"确立\"，反了。\n借⽤原⽂：stop looking at responses to the mirror as a decisive test\nB) 过度推断\n录音满是 self-awareness，选项 advanced the concept of self-awareness to a higher\nlevel 盗用这个词造\"把自我意识概念提升到更高层次\"，听着很顺，但录音说的是结果带来更\n多疑问、要换更丰富的理论，并未声称\"提升了概念层次\"。\n借⽤原⽂：Only with a richer theory of the self and a larger test battery will we be able to\ndetermine all of the various levels of self-awareness\nC) 未提及\n选项 incorporate more perspectives than one 和正确项 raise more questions than\nanswers 都是 more…than… 句式，长得像；但录音结果说的是\"问题比答案多\"，没说\"纳入\n多个视角\"，C 是借相似句式硬凑、原文无对应。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-12-2-l-19", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 19, "stem": "What finding from the new study might come as a huge surprise?", "options": ["A) Small children are even more keen on screens than their elders.", "B) Both parents and children preferred to read electronic books.", "C) Parents differed from children in reading behavior.", "D) Children reading electronic books learned more."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） Children reading electronic books learned more.\n【定位】So the finding from a new study that very young children enjoy electronic books more than\nprinted picture books may not come as a huge surprise — but the following additional\nfindings might: both parents and children behaved differently when reading electronic versus\nprinted picture books. And the children who read the electronic books learned more.\n因此，一项新研究发现幼儿比起纸质图画书更喜欢电子书，这一点也许并不令人意外——但\n接下来这些额外发现就可能令人意外了：父母和孩子在读电子书与纸质书时表现各不相同；\n而且读电子书的孩子学到的更多。\n【信号】转折 but…might 指向真正的意外。录音先用 may not come as a huge surprise 把\"小孩\n爱电子书\"排掉，紧接 but the following additional findings might 一个转折，明确告诉\n你\"意外的发现\"在 but 之后；冒号后并列两条，其中 the children who read the\nelectronic books learned more 正是答案落点。\n【替换】the children who read the electronic books learned more ↔\nChildren reading electronic books learned more\n近乎同义、仅句式微调\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nSmall children are even more keen on screens than their elders. 借用了录音 small\nchildren are generally keen on screens 的原词，但录音说这是\"也许并不令人意外\"的前\n提，与题面要的\"意外发现\"正相反，听到 keen on screens 就选会中招。\n借⽤原⽂：small children are generally keen on screens\nB) 偷换概念\nBoth parents and children preferred to read electronic books. 套用了录音 both\nparents and children behaved differently 里的 both parents and children，却把\nbehaved differently（表现不同）偷换成 preferred to read electronic books（都偏爱电\n子书），原文从没说父母也偏爱电子书。\n借⽤原⽂：both parents and children behaved differently\nC) 答非所问\nParents differed from children in reading behavior. 确实对应 both parents and\nchildren behaved differently，本身没说错，但题干特别问\"令人意外\"的发现，录音的\nsurprise 落点落在\"孩子学得更多\"上；C 是 but 后并列的发现之一，却不是题面聚焦的那一\n条，被更贴题的 D 比下去。\n借⽤原⽂：both parents and children behaved differently", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-12-2-l-20", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 20, "stem": "What do we learn about previous studies of young children reading electronic books?", "options": ["A) They came to conflicting conclusions.", "B) They were based on small samples.", "C) They yielded unanticipated findings.", "D) They were focused on younger kids."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） They came to conflicting conclusions.\n【定位】In fact, multiple studies have found that electronic books support story comprehension and\nvocabulary gains beyond what is provided by printed books. But there's also work suggesting\nthat children sometimes invest less mental effort in learning from electronic books, and that\nparents talk less about content when sharing such books.\n事实上，多项研究发现，电子书在故事理解和词汇增长上的助益超过纸质书。但也有研究表\n明，孩子有时在电子书上投入的脑力反而更少，父母在一起读这类书时谈论内容也更少。\n【信号】but 把两拨研究结论正反对置。录音先说 multiple studies have found that electronic\nbooks support story comprehension and vocabulary gains（电子书有利），再用 But\nthere's also work suggesting that children sometimes invest less mental effort（也有\n研究说不利）一转，正反两拨研究结论摆在一起——这正是\"结论相互矛盾\"的信号。\n【替换】multiple studies have found that electronic books support story comprehension and vocabulary gains\n↔ They came to conflicting conclusions\n正反两面概括为\"矛盾\"\n【排除】B) 未提及\nThey were based on small samples. 谈以往研究的样本量，但录音讲 previous studies\n时只对比了结论方向，全程没说过样本大小，属凭\"评价研究常谈样本\"的常识硬填。\nC) 张冠李戴\nThey yielded unanticipated findings. 里 unanticipated 蹭了第 1 段 huge surprise\n的\"意外\"概念，但那是形容\"本次新研究\"的发现；本题问的是 previous studies（以往研\n究），张冠李戴。\n借⽤原⽂：but the following additional findings might\nD) 偷换概念\nThey were focused on younger kids. 借了录音反复出现的 young children 概念，但录音\n说的是\"也有针对 older children 的研究\"，并未限定以往研究都只盯更小的孩子，把研究对\n象范围说窄了。\n借⽤原⽂：there's research on older children", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-12-2-l-21", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 21, "stem": "What extra features should be included in electronic books to bolster children's learning?", "options": ["A) Those that prove simple enough for them to comprehend.", "B) Those that produce both audio and visual effects on them.", "C) Those that draw their attention to the educational content.", "D) Those that contribute to their increased interest in reading."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） Those that draw their attention to the educational content.\n【定位】Extra features in electronic books may increase children's participation with them; however, if\nthese features don't draw attention to the educational content, they may not serve as a\nsupportive feature.\n电子书里的额外功能也许能提高孩子的参与度；然而，如果这些功能不能把注意力引向教育\n内容，它们就可能起不到辅助作用。\n【信号】however + if 否定句给出条件。录音先承认 Extra features…may increase children's\nparticipation，再用 however, if these features don't draw attention to the\neducational content, they may not serve as a supportive feature 反转——用\"如果不\n指向教育内容就没用\"反推出\"要指向教育内容才有用\"，答案落在 if 否定句里。\n【替换】if these features don't draw attention to the educational content, they may not serve as a supportive\nfeature\n↔ Those that draw their attention to the educational content\n否定条件转正面、原词点对\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nThose that prove simple enough for them to comprehend. 蹭了第 5 段 the electronic\nbooks in the study were simple 的 simple，但那只是描述研究所用电子书的特点，录音\n从没说\"简单到能懂\"是促进学习该加的功能，听到 simple 就选会落坑。\n借⽤原⽂：the electronic books in the study were simple\nB) 原词陷阱\nThose that produce both audio and visual effects on them. 直接搬录音 they included\nbackground music, animation and sound effects 的音乐、动画、音效，这些恰是\"声光\n效果\"，但录音的落点是\"光有这些额外功能、若不指向教育内容就没用\"，B 把被否定的诱饵\n当成了答案。\n借⽤原⽂：they included background music, animation and sound effects\nD) 偷换概念\nThose that contribute to their increased interest in reading. 像是改写自录音 Extra\nfeatures…may increase children's participation，但录音说的是 increase participation\n（提高参与度），并把它和\"促进学习\"区分开（参与高未必学得好），选项把它换成\nincreased interest in reading（提高阅读兴趣）且当成促学习的条件，偷换且跑题。\n借⽤原⽂：Extra features in electronic books may increase children's participation with them", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-12-2-l-22", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 22, "stem": "What should we do with the unfavorable decisions we've made?", "options": ["A) Regret having wasted much time in making them.", "B) Think how different we could have made them.", "C) Keep ourselves from regretting them.", "D) Live with them for the time being."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） Keep ourselves from regretting them.\n【定位】If you're living your life full of regrets, you're wasting your time. ... There are plenty of\nunfavorable decisions we've made, but does that mean we should regret them? No!\n如果你的人生充满后悔，那就是在浪费时间。……我们确实做过很多不理想的决定，但这是\n否意味着我们该为之后悔呢？不！\n【信号】反问加 No 的强烈否定。does that mean we should regret them? No! 用'反问 + 斩钉截\n铁的 No'把态度顶到最高：不该后悔。这种自问自答后紧跟否定的句子，正是演讲者亮出主\n张的答案信号。\n【替换】does that mean we should regret them? No! ↔ Keep ourselves from regretting them\n'不该后悔'＝'避免后悔'\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nA 把原文里 living your life full of regrets, you're wasting your time 的两个原词 regret\n和 wasting time 拼到一起，做成 Regret having wasted much time in making them.，\n但原文说'后悔本身是浪费时间'，A 却变成'为浪费时间而后悔'，听到原词就选必中招。\n借⽤原⽂：living your life full of regrets, you're wasting your time\nB) 与原文相反\n原文 just because things could be different, it doesn't necessarily mean they would\nbe better 是在否定'设想不同会更好'，B 项 Think how different we could have made\nthem. 却把这件被否定的事当成'应该做的'，正话反说，与原意相左。\n借⽤原⽂：just because things could be different, it doesn't necessarily mean they would be better\nD) 未提及\nLive with them for the time being. 主张'暂且与这些决定共处'，但原文从头到尾讲的是'别\n后悔、向前看'，并没有说'暂且忍受'这种过渡性态度，属于无中生有。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-12-2-l-23", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 23, "stem": "What does the speaker say about every single decision we've ever made?", "options": ["A) It has made us the person we are now.", "B) It has been made with a lot of thinking.", "C) It stems from our valuable knowledge.", "D) It benefits us greatly one way or another."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） It has made us the person we are now.\n【定位】Every single decision you've ever made, positive or negative, has made you the person you\nare today.\n你做过的每一个决定，无论好坏，都造就了今天的你。\n【信号】题干原词在句首直接成句。原文 Every single decision you've ever made, positive or\nnegative, has made you the person you are today 句首即题干原话 every single\ndecision，紧接着的 has made you the person you are today 就是对它的评价，是直接\n成句的答案信号。\n【替换】has made you the person you are today ↔ It has made us the person we are now\n几乎同义、仅人称与 today/now 替换\n【排除】B) 未提及\nIt has been made with a lot of thinking. 说决定是'经过大量思考做出的'，但原文评价决\n定时说的是它'造就了今天的你'，并没有谈决定是否深思熟虑，属无中生有。\nC) 原词陷阱\nC 搬来原文 the valuable knowledge from the lessons we learned 里的 valuable\nknowledge，做成 It stems from our valuable knowledge.，但原文是说'回到过去会失去\n这份宝贵知识'，并非说决定'源自'知识，盗原词且因果方向反了。\n借⽤原⽂：the valuable knowledge from the lessons we learned\nD) 答非所问\nIt benefits us greatly one way or another. 说决定'总能大大有益'，虽与全篇基调沾边，\n但原文评价 every single decision 的具体说法是'造就了今天的你'，D 是脱离答案句的笼统\n概括，并非演讲者此处的原话。\n借⽤原⽂：Every single decision you've ever made, positive or negative, has made you the person\nyou are today", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-12-2-l-24", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 24, "stem": "What is life full of according to the speaker?", "options": ["A) Challenging things to do.", "B) Beautiful things to share.", "C) Choices to make.", "D) Lessons to learn."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） Lessons to learn.\n【定位】Life is full of lessons to learn, so we can't let regret stop us from moving forward.\n人生充满了需要汲取的教训，所以我们不能让后悔阻挡自己前进。\n【信号】题干句式原样出现。原文 Life is full of lessons to learn 与题干句式 full of 一字不差，紧\n跟其后的 lessons to learn 即是要填的中心词，是句式复刻型的答案信号。\n【替换】Life is full of lessons to learn ↔ Lessons to learn\n原句中心词直接对应选项\n【排除】C) 原词陷阱\nChoices to make. 借了原文反复出现的 the choices you do 里的 choices，与 decisions\n近义、特别像，但 full of 后面原文念的是 lessons to learn 不是 choices，听到近义词就选\n会被换走。\n借⽤原⽂：the choices you do\nA) 未提及\nChallenging things to do. 形式上凑成并列项，但原文 full of 后并没有念 challenging\nthings，演讲者也没说人生充满挑战，属于未提及。\nB) 未提及\nBeautiful things to share. 听起来很正能量，但原文 full of 后是 lessons to learn，从未\n提'美好的可分享之物'，靠基调相近蒙人。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-12-2-l-25", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 25, "stem": "What is said to be of real importance when we make poor decisions in life?", "options": ["A) Foreseeing the possibility for regret later.", "B) Making proper use of the lessons learned.", "C) Perfecting the decision-making strategies.", "D) Seeing them as obstacles to moving forward."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） Making proper use of the lessons learned.\n【定位】You will make poor decisions in life — we all do — but it's what you do with the lessons\nlearned that matters.\n你在人生中会做出糟糕的决定——我们都会——但真正重要的，是你如何运用从中学到的\n教训。\n【信号】but 转折 + that matters 强调。but it's what you do with the lessons learned that\nmatters 既有转折 but 把重点拉到后半句，又用 it's…that matters 的强调结构点出'真正要\n紧的'，双重信号锁定答案句。\n【替换】what you do with the lessons learned that matters ↔ Making proper use of the lessons learned\n'如何运用教训'＝'恰当运用教训'\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nA 搬来原文 there will always be a possibility for regret later 几乎整句，做成\nForeseeing the possibility for regret later.，但原文这半句只是陈述'总会有后悔的可能'，\n并不是 that matters 强调的'真正重要的事'，盗整句却答非所问。\n借⽤原⽂：there will always be a possibility for regret later\nC) 未提及\nPerfecting the decision-making strategies. 说要'完善决策策略'，听着合理，但原文强调\n的是'用好已学到的教训'，并没有让你去优化决策方法，属无中生有。\nD) 与原文相反\nD 搬来原文 we can't let regret stop us from moving forward 里的 moving forward 与\nobstacle 之意，做成 Seeing them as obstacles to moving forward.，但原文是'别让后\n悔挡住前进'，演讲者恰恰反对把决定当障碍，D 把意思反了。\n借⽤原⽂：we can't let regret stop us from moving forward", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-12-2-c-26", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 26, "stem": "", "options": ["A) accompanying", "B) cognitive", "C) domains", "D) entertaining", "E) evoking", "F) hybrid", "G) impetus", "H) intuitive", "I) once", "J) oversee", "K) redundant", "L) stances", "M) surpass", "N) tentatively", "O) vanish"], "answer": "I", "analysis": "【答案】I) once\n副词\n【词性槽】主语与谓语之间的状语位\n【依据】与 but 后的 has shown 构成今昔对比\nPeople __(26)__ thought that humans were the only animals to use tools, to form\nteams or to propagate culture, but science has shown that other animals do each of\nthese things. 前半句过去时 thought、后半句现在完成时 has shown，典型 \"过去曾以为 /\n如今已证明\" 结构，填 once（曾经）正好咬合这条时间逻辑。\n【竞争词】N) tentatively 语义指向不对，是 \"试探性地\" 而非 \"曾经\"\ntentatively 意为 \"暂时地、试探性地\"，强调态度不坚定。但原文说人们 \"长久以为人类是唯一会\n用工具的动物\"，这是一种**坚信**而非试探，与下句被 but 反转的语气不匹配。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·主语与谓语之间的状语位"]}, {"id": "2025-12-2-c-27", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 27, "stem": "", "options": ["A) accompanying", "B) cognitive", "C) domains", "D) entertaining", "E) evoking", "F) hybrid", "G) impetus", "H) intuitive", "I) once", "J) oversee", "K) redundant", "L) stances", "M) surpass", "N) tentatively", "O) vanish"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B) cognitive\n形容词\n【词性槽】名词 tasks 前的前置定语\n【依据】与 \"require a human brain\" 同义呼应\nclaims that __(27)__ tasks require a human brain——能 require a human brain（需要\n人脑）的任务，正是 cognitive tasks（认知性的任务、需要思维加工的任务）。下一句又\n用 adding machine、computer-generated art、computer matches human\nintelligence 反复指向 \"智力/认知\"，B 与上下文构成最稳同义网。\n【竞争词】H) intuitive \"直觉的\" 与 \"人脑认知\" 范围不符\nintuitive 指 \"凭直觉的\"，强调非理性、不依靠分析。而本段下文举例（加法机、AI 绘画、计算\n机匹敌人类智能）恰恰是**理性认知**任务被机器接管，与 intuitive 的语义指向相反。\nD) entertaining \"有趣的\" 偏娱乐，与 \"需要人脑\" 不沾边\nentertaining 指 \"令人愉快的、有娱乐性的\"，与 require a human brain（需要人脑）这一论点\n完全脱节，纯属词性诱饵。\nK) redundant \"多余的任务\" 与原意南辕北辙\nredundant 是 \"多余的、被裁掉的\"——\"多余的任务需要人脑\" 在逻辑上根本说不通；况且 K 后\n文 30 题正等着它。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 H) —— 看到 AI 话题就联想 \"直觉\"。intuitive 词性对、又与 AI 话\n题表面沾边（\"机器没有直觉\"），最易让考生误填。防坑：claims that __(27)__ tasks\nrequire a human brain 里同句锚词是 a human brain，\"需要人脑的任务\" = cognitive\n（认知性的），而非 intuitive（直觉的）。\"找同句同义锚\" 是六级选词填空的必修招式。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·名词 tasks 前的前置定语"]}, {"id": "2025-12-2-c-28", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 28, "stem": "", "options": ["A) accompanying", "B) cognitive", "C) domains", "D) entertaining", "E) evoking", "F) hybrid", "G) impetus", "H) intuitive", "I) once", "J) oversee", "K) redundant", "L) stances", "M) surpass", "N) tentatively", "O) vanish"], "answer": "M", "analysis": "【答案】M) surpass\n动词\n【词性槽】and 并列谓语：computers meet and __ human intelligence\n【依据】\"匹敌并超越\" 是 AI 论述的固定搭配\nI believe the moment when computers meet and __(28)__ human intelligence is on\nthe horizon.——meet（达到/匹敌）+ surpass（超越）是 AI 话题里最常见的递进搭配，\n且呼应第三段 when machines become smarter and more creative than the brightest\npeople（变得比最聪明的人更聪明、更有创造力），证实方向就是 \"超越\"。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·and 并列谓语：computers m"]}, {"id": "2025-12-2-c-29", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 29, "stem": "", "options": ["A) accompanying", "B) cognitive", "C) domains", "D) entertaining", "E) evoking", "F) hybrid", "G) impetus", "H) intuitive", "I) once", "J) oversee", "K) redundant", "L) stances", "M) surpass", "N) tentatively", "O) vanish"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C) domains\n名词\n【词性槽】介词 In some 后的名词复数\n【依据】\"在某些领域里\" 与下文 In other fields 形成对照\n下一段开头 In other fields, human skill will seem costly and __(30)__. 用 In other fields\n与本段 In some __(29)__ 形成 \"在某些领域 / 在另一些领域\" 的并列对照；domains（领\n域）与 fields 同义，正好咬合这条段间对照逻辑。\n【竞争词】L) stances \"立场\" 与下文 fields 不对应\nstances 指 \"立场、姿态\"，是抽象观点。但下段 in other fields 明确把对比维度定在 \"领域\" 而\n非 \"立场\"——填 stances 会让段间对照失锚。\nG) impetus 形态不对：单数且不可数\nimpetus 通常作不可数单数（推动力），与 some + 复数名词的结构不合；语义上 \"在某些推动\n力里人们仍重视人类\" 也不通。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 L) —— 复数名词形态一致的假同义。stances 与 domains 同为复\n数名词、同能套进 In some __ 结构，最易混。防坑：盯紧下段段首 In other fields——\"领\n域 vs 立场\" 是两条线，fields 同义对应 domains。\"段间对照锁名词\" 一招直接破。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·介词 In some 后的名词复数"]}, {"id": "2025-12-2-c-30", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 30, "stem": "", "options": ["A) accompanying", "B) cognitive", "C) domains", "D) entertaining", "E) evoking", "F) hybrid", "G) impetus", "H) intuitive", "I) once", "J) oversee", "K) redundant", "L) stances", "M) surpass", "N) tentatively", "O) vanish"], "answer": "K", "analysis": "【答案】K) redundant\n形容词\n【词性槽】并列连词 and 后接形容词，与 costly 并列\n【依据】\"昂贵 + 多余\" 共同描述被淘汰的人类技能\n本段例 most readers don't care whether the graphic __(31)__ a magazine article was\ndrawn by a person or a computer——读者并不在意是人画的还是机器画的，意味着人类\n技能既贵又**没必要**，redundant（多余的、被裁掉的）正好把这层负面评价点透。\n【竞争词】D) entertaining 褒义色彩与 costly 不同向\nentertaining（有趣的）偏褒，而 costly 偏负面——并列时色彩不一致，逻辑上 \"昂贵且有趣\"\n也无法描述被市场淘汰的人类劳动。\nH) intuitive \"直觉的\" 与 \"被淘汰\" 主线不沾\nintuitive 指 \"直觉的\"，并不直接传递价值高低、是否被取代的信息，与本段 \"人类技能将变得不\n值\" 的论点错位。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·并列连词 and 后接形容词，与 cos"]}, {"id": "2025-12-2-c-31", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 31, "stem": "", "options": ["A) accompanying", "B) cognitive", "C) domains", "D) entertaining", "E) evoking", "F) hybrid", "G) impetus", "H) intuitive", "I) once", "J) oversee", "K) redundant", "L) stances", "M) surpass", "N) tentatively", "O) vanish"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A) accompanying\n形容词\n【词性槽】the graphic __ a magazine article：现在分词作后置定语\n【依据】\"配文插图\" 是固定搭配\nthe graphic __(31)__ a magazine article was drawn by a person or a computer——\ngraphic accompanying a magazine article 即 \"配在杂志文章旁的插图\"，accompany 与\ngraphic/article 是新闻出版中的常规搭配。\n【竞争词】E) evoking \"唤起一篇文章\" 搭配不成立\nevoking 是 \"唤起、引发\"，搭配对象多是 emotion/memory 等抽象名词；evoke a magazine\narticle（唤起一篇杂志文章）语义不通，逻辑上也不是 \"插图与文章\" 的关系。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·the graphic __ a mag"]}, {"id": "2025-12-2-c-32", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 32, "stem": "", "options": ["A) accompanying", "B) cognitive", "C) domains", "D) entertaining", "E) evoking", "F) hybrid", "G) impetus", "H) intuitive", "I) once", "J) oversee", "K) redundant", "L) stances", "M) surpass", "N) tentatively", "O) vanish"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D) entertaining\n形容词\n【词性槽】and 并列形容词链：relevant, new and __\n【依据】读者期待 \"贴题、新颖、有趣\" 三件套\nthey just want it to be relevant, new and perhaps __(32)__.——读者作为大众，对杂志\n插图的诉求是 relevant（与文章相关）、new（新鲜）、entertaining（有趣）这三件套，\n构成同色彩并列；perhaps 又留了余地，正合 \"有趣\" 这种偏锦上添花的属性。\n【竞争词】H) intuitive \"直觉的\" 与并列项不同色彩\nintuitive 偏认知/操作领域（如 intuitive interface 易上手的界面），与读者读杂志插图时的诉\n求错位；与 relevant、new 也无法构成同色彩并列。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·and 并列形容词链：relevant,"]}, {"id": "2025-12-2-c-33", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 33, "stem": "", "options": ["A) accompanying", "B) cognitive", "C) domains", "D) entertaining", "E) evoking", "F) hybrid", "G) impetus", "H) intuitive", "I) once", "J) oversee", "K) redundant", "L) stances", "M) surpass", "N) tentatively", "O) vanish"], "answer": "F", "analysis": "【答案】F) hybrid\n名词\n【词性槽】a + __ 单数可数名词位\n【依据】where 从句直接定义 \"人机混合\"\nMany fields will be a __(33)__, where some humans find a lucky place, but most of\nthe work is done by computers, like in manufacturing today, where much of the\nwork is accomplished by robots, but some humans must be there to __(34)__ the\nmachines.——where 从句里 \"some humans … but most of the work is done by\ncomputers\" 把空名词定义成 \"人机混合体\"，hybrid 是唯一精准对应词。\n【竞争词】G) impetus \"推动力\" 与 \"人机混合\" 错位\nimpetus 是 \"推动力、动力\"，无法被 where 从句中 \"既有人又有计算机\" 的描述合理修饰；\"许\n多领域将成为一种推动力\" 在语义上也不通。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·a + __ 单数可数名词位"]}, {"id": "2025-12-2-c-34", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 34, "stem": "", "options": ["A) accompanying", "B) cognitive", "C) domains", "D) entertaining", "E) evoking", "F) hybrid", "G) impetus", "H) intuitive", "I) once", "J) oversee", "K) redundant", "L) stances", "M) surpass", "N) tentatively", "O) vanish"], "answer": "J", "analysis": "【答案】J) oversee\n动词\n【词性槽】must be there to + 动词原形\n【依据】\"机器为主、人来监管\" 正是本段图景\nmuch of the work is accomplished by robots, but some humans must be there to\n__(34)__ the machines. 转折 but 之后明确：活儿主要由机器人干，但仍要有人 \"在\n场\"——\"在场对机器\" 干什么？最贴合的就是 \"监督、监管\" oversee，与制造业现实经验也\n一致。\n【竞争词】M) surpass \"超越机器\" 与本段 \"人辅助机器\" 逻辑反向\nsurpass（超越）会把句意变成 \"人必须在场以超越机器\"，与 but 引出的 \"机器为主、人为辅\"\n的图景反向；况且 surpass 已是 28 题的答案。\nO) vanish 不及物动词不能带 machines\nvanish 是不及物动词（消失），其后不能直接接 the machines 作宾语；语义上 \"人必须在那里\n以消失机器\" 也不通。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·must be there to + 动"]}, {"id": "2025-12-2-c-35", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 35, "stem": "", "options": ["A) accompanying", "B) cognitive", "C) domains", "D) entertaining", "E) evoking", "F) hybrid", "G) impetus", "H) intuitive", "I) once", "J) oversee", "K) redundant", "L) stances", "M) surpass", "N) tentatively", "O) vanish"], "answer": "O", "analysis": "【答案】O) vanish\n动词\n【词性槽】cause more jobs to + 动词原形\n【依据】AI 会让更多工作消失，对应全段悲观预测\nadvances in AI will cause more jobs to __(35)__, that creative workers with human-\nonly skills will become richer but fewer in number——后半 \"人类技能工作者会变少\"\n直接揭示前半 \"更多工作 __\" 的方向是 \"消失\"，vanish 正中靶心。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·cause more jobs to +"]}, {"id": "2025-12-2-m-36", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 36, "stem": "Feeling like they have more control over something or someone than they do, people get more committed to their pursuit when they should give up.", "options": null, "answer": "F", "analysis": "【答案】F 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】高估掌控感导致继续投入\n【定位】Especially when combined with an \"I'm not a quitter\" mentality, feeling like we have more\ncontrol over an event or person than we do puts us at risk of doubling down when we should\npull out.\n尤其当与\"我不是放弃者\"的心态结合时，觉得自己对某件事或某个人有比实际更多的掌控\n感，会让我们在本该退出时反而加码投入。\n【改写】Feeling like they have more control over something or someone than they do ↔\nfeeling like we have more control over an event or person than we do\n代词与名词的同义替换\nget more committed to their pursuit when they should give up ↔\ndoubling down when we should pull out\n口语短语的解释式改写", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2025-12-2-m-37", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 37, "stem": "If you regard yourself as one who never quits, you may end up trapped in your course of action.", "options": null, "answer": "K", "analysis": "【答案】K 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】自认从不放弃者会被困住\n【定位】If you strongly identify as not being a quitter, you can end up staying stuck in a course of\naction due to your narrow and rigid self-concept.\n如果你强烈地把自己认同为不是一个放弃者，那么你会因为狭隘僵化的自我概念，最终被困\n在某一行动路线里。\n【改写】If you regard yourself as one who never quits ↔ If you strongly identify as not being a quitter\n动词与短语同义替换\ntrapped in your course of action ↔ staying stuck in a course of action\n近义动词同义替换\n【辨邻】 F F段出现了I'm not a quitter这一相同表述，容易让人误以为是源头段；但它的核心论\n点是控制错觉与该心态叠加导致doubling down，并不是题干所说的trapped in your\ncourse of action。\nEspecially when combined with an \"I'm not a quitter\" mentality, feeling like we have more control\nover an event or person than we do puts us at risk of doubling down when we should pull out.", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2025-12-2-m-38", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 38, "stem": "One of the author's clients found it hard to give up a design project, in which she had put in a lot of time and effort.", "options": null, "answer": "J", "analysis": "【答案】J 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】客户与设计项目的难放手\n【定位】Ryan had invested two years and a lot of effort in a design project that wasn't delivering the\nneeded outcomes, but the thought of abandoning it after all she had put in was hard to bear.\nRyan在一个迟迟拿不出预期成果的设计项目上投入了两年时间和大量精力，但一想到要在投\n入这么多之后放弃它就难以承受。\n【改写】One of the author's clients ↔ my client Ryan\n专名与泛指对应\nfound it hard to give up a design project ↔\nthe thought of abandoning it after all she had put in was hard to bear\n形容词短语同义替换\nin which she had put in a lot of time and effort ↔\ninvested two years and a lot of effort in a design project\n具体时长概括为时间\n【辨邻】 P P段同样提到leading the design project，但内容是Ryan从中获得new skills and\nleadership capabilities并感激这段经历；它叙述的是后续学习与释怀，不是题干强调\n的found it hard to give up这一时刻。\nRyan recognized she had developed various new skills and leadership capabilities from leading the\ndesign project.", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2025-12-2-m-39", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 39, "stem": "The author suggests that one should direct their thoughts to the gains of quitting timely so as to move on.", "options": null, "answer": "E", "analysis": "【答案】E 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】把思考转向及时放弃的收益\n【定位】To offset this bias, deliberately refocus your thinking on the gains so you can more\nobjectively weigh the alternatives.\n为抵消这一偏差，要刻意把思考重心转向收益，这样你才能更客观地权衡各种选择。\n【改写】direct their thoughts to the gains ↔ refocus your thinking on the gains\n动词与名词同义替换\nthe gains of quitting timely ↔ What might I gain by cutting my losses now?\n及时止损的同义表达\nso as to move on ↔ so you can more objectively weigh the alternatives\n目的状语的概括式改写", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2025-12-2-m-40", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 40, "stem": "When making a decision, you'd better ask for opinions of those who have less stake in your decision.", "options": null, "answer": "M", "analysis": "【答案】M 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】向利益相关度低的人征询\n【定位】Ideally, seek out people who are less invested in your decision than you are.\n理想情况下，要找那些比你在这项决定中投入度更低的人征询意见。\n【改写】ask for opinions of those who have less stake in your decision ↔\nseek out people who are less invested in your decision than you are\n动词与利益关系词同义\n【辨邻】 L L段只说seek external sources of information, rather than relying solely on your\nthinking and instincts并提示要think through whose opinions you solicit，把谁来\n征询这一具体回答推到下一段；它没有less stake这层意思，因此不是源头段。\nIt's also helpful to seek external sources of information, rather than relying solely on your thinking\nand instincts. But it's important to think through whose opinions you solicit.", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2025-12-2-m-41", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 41, "stem": "For people associating their identity with their commitments, quitting can feel like their identity is threatened.", "options": null, "answer": "I", "analysis": "【答案】I 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】身份与承诺挂钩\n【定位】Research indicates that people link their self-identity and social status to their commitments.\nAnd because we identify with our commitments, withdrawing from one can feel like a threat\nto our identity or status.\n研究表明，人们会把自我认同与社会地位与自己的承诺挂钩。正因为我们把自己等同于这些\n承诺，退出其中一项就会让人觉得像是对身份或地位的威胁。\n【改写】associating their identity with their commitments ↔\npeople link their self-identity and social status to their commitments\n动词同义替换\nquitting can feel like their identity is threatened ↔\nwithdrawing from one can feel like a threat to our identity or status\n动名词被动改写", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2025-12-2-m-42", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 42, "stem": "People who regard highly the quality of making wise decisions do not feel good when they make a bad one.", "options": null, "answer": "O", "analysis": "【答案】O 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】看重明智决策者面对坏决定的感受\n【定位】Nobody likes to feel that they've made a poor decision, especially if making intelligent\ndecisions is a quality you hold in high esteem.\n没有人喜欢觉得自己做了一个糟糕的决定，尤其当你把做出明智决定视为自己很看重的品\n质时。\n【改写】regard highly the quality of making wise decisions ↔\nmaking intelligent decisions is a quality you hold in high esteem\n动词短语与形容词同义\ndo not feel good when they make a bad one ↔\nNobody likes to feel that they've made a poor decision\n否定改写+近义形容词", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2025-12-2-m-43", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 43, "stem": "The wisest leaders learn to distinguish between giving up too soon and sticking with something irrationally.", "options": null, "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】最聪明领导者懂得分辨\n【定位】But the smartest leaders learn to discern the difference between quitting too soon and holding\nonto something that they shouldn't.\n但最聪明的领导者会学着分辨过早放弃与不该坚持却继续坚持之间的差别。\n【改写】The wisest leaders ↔ the smartest leaders\n近义形容词最高级\ndistinguish between giving up too soon and sticking with something irrationally ↔\ndiscern the difference between quitting too soon and holding onto something that they\nshouldn't\n动词与短语的同义概括\n【辨邻】 Q Q段出现learn how to distinguish quitting too soon and clinging to a losing\ncourse of action这一与题干高度相似的并置，是最容易让人误选的相邻段；但它谈的\n对象是普遍意义的人，并未限定The wisest leaders这一主语，故不是源头段。\nBut for your well-being, it's important to learn how to distinguish quitting too soon and clinging to\na losing course of action.", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2025-12-2-m-44", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 44, "stem": "When people are thinking of an endeavor, they should dig out reliable data that tells them how likely they are to succeed.", "options": null, "answer": "N", "analysis": "【答案】N 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】用可靠数据评估成功概率\n【定位】Alternately, you could also seek out hard data that illuminates your real chance of success in\nwhatever endeavor you are contemplating.\n或者，你也可以去寻找能够照亮你成功真实概率的硬数据，无论你在考虑哪种事业。\n【改写】dig out reliable data ↔ seek out hard data\n动词与形容词同义\nhow likely they are to succeed ↔ your real chance of success\n名词短语改写为从句\nWhen people are thinking of an endeavor ↔ in whatever endeavor you are contemplating\n近义动词替换", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2025-12-2-m-45", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 45, "stem": "Having a clear view of what's in your control and what's not is vital to making a good decision regarding whether to give up or stick it out.", "options": null, "answer": "H", "analysis": "【答案】H 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】看清可控与否对决策至关重要\n【定位】Getting clear on what you can control and what you can't is essential to making a quality\ndecision about whether to call it quits or persevere.\n把自己能控制什么、不能控制什么搞清楚，对于做出关于该放弃还是该坚持的高质量决定至\n关重要。\n【改写】Having a clear view of what's in your control and what's not ↔\nGetting clear on what you can control and what you can't\n动名词短语同义\nvital to making a good decision ↔ essential to making a quality decision\n近义形容词同义\nwhether to give up or stick it out ↔ whether to call it quits or persevere\n并置短语整体同义\n【辨邻】 G G段聚焦工具：make a simple two-column list of what's in your control and\nwhat's not；它没有vital to making a good decision这层论点，因此不是源头段。\nTo counteract this bias, consider your situation and make a simple two-column list of what's in your\ncontrol and what's not.\nPARAGRAPH MAP · 段落主旨图\n个段落各讲了什么\n逐段一句主旨 + 该段最具区分度的「信息指纹」，是逐句回扫定位的工作台。\nA\n开场提出问题：项目、关系、工作并不总按计划进行，何时放弃并不清晰，并引出俗\n语quitters never win与有时止损才是上策的张力。\n信息指纹 俗语引入与止损主张\nB\n把抽象问题落到生活实例：人们抓住已结束的关系或痛苦的工作不放，指出毅力虽好\n但可能让人在不再可行的事上over-invest。\n信息指纹 毅力的两面性\nC\n作者亮身份As an executive coach，并提出最聪明的领导者会学着分辨the difference\nbetween quitting too soon and holding onto something that they shouldn't。\n信息指纹 最聪明领导者的辨别能力\nD\n引入第一个偏差——sunk-cost delusion：因已投入时间、金钱、精力而继续做明明该\n停的事；并指出自认为不轻易放弃的人尤其易中招。\n信息指纹 沉没成本错觉的定义\nE 针对沉没成本错觉给出对策：把注意力主动转向refocus your thinking on the gains，\n权衡及时止损的收益与继续硬扛的代价。\n信息指纹 把思考重心转向止损收益\nF 引入第二个偏差——The illusion of control：高估自己掌控事件与他人的能力；当它和\nI'm not a quitter心态叠加，就会让人本该退出时反而加码。\n信息指纹 控制错觉与加码风险\nG 对策：列一个two-column list of what's in your control and what's not，严肃区分可\n控与不可控，并指出你无法强迫他人或环境改变。\n信息指纹 可控/不可控两栏清单\nH 把可控/不可控分清是做出去留高质量决策的essential前提；有了清单才能聚焦真正能\n控制的事，并评估继续投入是否值得。\n信息指纹 分清可控与否对决策至关重要\nI 研究表明人们把自我认同与社会地位绑在自己的commitments上；因此放弃一项承诺\n会让人感到a threat to our identity or status。\n信息指纹 承诺与身份/地位绑定\nJ 用客户Ryan的例子：她在一个设计项目里投入了two years and a lot of effort却看不到\n成效；她以I'm not a quitter自我设限，判断力受蒙蔽。\n信息指纹 客户Ryan的两年设计项目\nK 解释身份认同的内在障碍：若强烈认同not being a quitter，会被狭隘僵化的自\n我概念困在原地；对策是回想自己其他正面品质，比如Ryan想到的Discernment\nand bravery。\n信息指纹 僵化自我概念把人困住\nL 进一步用这些品质的视角审视处境，并提出要寻求external sources of information，\n而非只依赖自己的想法和直觉，但要想清向谁征询意见。\n信息指纹 用不同品质视角看问题并求外部意见\nM 求外部意见时，要找people who are less invested in your decision than you are：例\n如考虑结束创业时找别的创始人而不是合伙人，恋爱问题别只问最好的朋友。\n信息指纹 找利益相关度低的人征询\nN 另一条路：去找hard data来照亮自己真正的成功概率；以创业为例，用烧钱速率、行\n业增长率等数字把自己从对点子的爱里拉出来。\n信息指纹 用硬数据评估成功概率\nO 当原本看好的事不如意，人容易overly self-critical，尤其当你把做明智决定看得很重\n时；对策是给自己一些self-compassion，提升韧性。\n信息指纹 过度自责与自我宽容的对策\nP 再回到Ryan：与其反复自责，不如关注从中学到了什么——她从设计项目里发展出新\n技能和领导力，即使最终放弃也庆幸尝试过。\n信息指纹 从经历中提取学习而非自责\nQ 结尾收束：毅力确能帮我们成功，但学会分辨quitting too soon and clinging to a\nlosing course of action同样重要；放手能腾出时间、精力、心理空间去追求新机会。\n信息指纹 全文收束与放手价值", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2025-12-2-r-46", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 46, "stem": "What does the author say about human beings?", "options": ["A) They cannot prosper without cooperating with each other.", "B) They share a common interest and believe in a just cause.", "C) They think and behave alike in different social groups.", "D) They cannot gain independence without fighting for it."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】What does the author say about human beings? 问的是作者就 human beings 这一具\n体对象说了什么，落点在某一具体陈述上而非整篇主旨。\n【拆句】What does the author say about human beings? 是常规疑问句结构，主干 \"the author\nsay\"，关于 human beings，无从句、无否定、无比较，结构平铺直叙，无陷阱。\n【定位】As interdependent beings we cannot thrive independent of others.\n作为相互依赖的存在，我们无法脱离他人独自繁荣兴盛。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nthrive ↔ prosper\ncannot thrive independent of others ↔ cannot prosper without cooperating with each other\nThey cannot prosper without cooperating with each other. 把 cannot thrive\nindependent of others 改写为\"必须合作才能兴旺\"，正是预判内容的同义转述。\nB) 偷换概念\nThey share a common interest and believe in a just cause. 取自 we share a\ncommon interest, or who also believe in a cause we are serious about，但原文这\n是\"喜欢和谁一起工作\"的举例，不是对 human beings 的总体陈述；且 cause 被偷换为\njust cause（正义事业）。\nC) 过度推断\nThey think and behave alike in different social groups. 声称\"在不同群体中想法行为\n都一样\"，原文只说人喜欢和\"like-minded\"的人共事，并未说人在所有群体中思想行为\n都相同。\nD) 与原文相反\nThey cannot gain independence without fighting for it. 谈\"独立要靠斗争争取\"，但\n原文 we cannot thrive independent of others 强调的是\"无法独立\"而非\"为独立而\n战\"，话题相反。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-12-2-r-47", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 47, "stem": "What do we learn about solidarity in an increasingly diverse society?", "options": ["A) It gives rise to a complicated division of labor.", "B) It involves many aspects with great complexity.", "C) It brings individuals together as environment changes.", "D) It embraces the characteristics of different individuals."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】What do we learn about solidarity in an increasingly diverse society? 提问限定在\"多元\n化社会下的 solidarity\"这一局部，并非整篇主旨。\n【拆句】What do we learn about solidarity in an increasingly diverse society? 主干 \"we learn\nabout solidarity\"，介词短语 \"in an increasingly diverse society\" 限定语境，无难句\n结构。\n【定位】In this increasingly diverse society we live in, solidarity takes on a complex and multifaceted\ncharacter as it forms bonds of interdependence among unique individuals living in a fast-\nchanging environment and working within a complex division of labor.\n在我们所处的这个日益多元化的社会里，solidarity 呈现出复杂而多面的特征，因为它在快\n速变化的环境中、在复杂的分工内部，于各不相同的个体之间形成了相互依赖的纽带。\n【选项】A) 强加因果\nIt gives rise to a complicated division of labor. 说 solidarity 导致了复杂分工，但原\n文 working within a complex division of labor 是说 solidarity 发生\"在\"复杂分工\"之\n内\"，分工是背景而非结果。\nB) ✓ 正确\ntakes on a complex and multifaceted character ↔\ninvolves many aspects with great complexity\ntakes on ↔ involves\ninvolves many aspects with great complexity 同时覆盖原文的 complex 和\nmultifaceted 两个核心修饰语，且未做任何额外延伸。\nC) 以偏概全\nIt brings individuals together as environment changes. 把\"形成相互依赖纽带\"窄化\n为\"把个体聚拢\"，且偷换了主线——题干问的是\"我们关于 solidarity 在多元社会中学到\n什么\"，正确答案应概括其\"特征\"，C 选了一处细节当主答。\nD) 偷换概念\nIt embraces the characteristics of different individuals. 说 solidarity\"接纳\"个体差\n异，但原文只说它在 unique individuals 之间形成纽带，并未说它\"接纳\"或\"包含\"个体\n特征。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-12-2-r-48", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 48, "stem": "What would we witness in the setting of communitarianism?", "options": ["A) Ruling of social norms and ridding of individuality.", "B) Emphasis on the adherence to individual differences.", "C) Sacrifice of personal interests for the common good.", "D) Elimination of individualism and personal choices."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】What would we witness in the setting of communitarianism? 问的是该情境下会观察到\n什么，指向文中具体描述句。\n【拆句】What would we witness in the setting of communitarianism? 主干 \"we would\nwitness\"，\"in the setting of communitarianism\" 限定情境，would 表示一般陈述语气，\n无难点。\n【定位】In this setting, individuals give up their own interests to serve the common good.\n在这种情境下，个人放弃自身利益以服务于共同利益。\n【选项】A) 以偏概全\nRuling of social norms and ridding of individuality. 把\"个体在某情境下放弃个人利\n益\"夸大为\"社会规范统治、个性被彻底清除\"，原文虽提到 individuality 被牺牲，但用的\n是 sacrificed（让位）而非 ridding（彻底清除）。\nB) 与原文相反\nEmphasis on the adherence to individual differences. 说社群主义强调坚持个体差\n异，但原文 strict adherence to social cohesiveness and exclusiveness restricts\nexpression and practice of individual differences 明说它限制了个体差异的表达，完\n全相反。\nC) ✓ 正确\nindividuals give up their own interests ↔ Sacrifice of personal interests\nto serve the common good ↔ for the common good\nSacrifice of personal interests for the common good. 是对 individuals give up\ntheir own interests to serve the common good 的完整、保守的名词化改写，与预判\n完全吻合。\nD) 过度推断\nElimination of individualism and personal choices. 用 Elimination（彻底消除）描述\n社群主义的效果，但原文只说 individuality and autonomy are sacrificed（让位/牺\n牲），并未达到\"彻底消除\"的程度。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-12-2-r-49", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 49, "stem": "What does solidarity entail according to the passage?", "options": ["A) A limit to personal differences.", "B) An opposition to individualism.", "C) A hindrance to social advancement.", "D) An inevitable end to liberal pursuits."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】What does solidarity entail according to the passage? 询问 solidarity 本质内涵，对应\n文中明确陈述句。\n【拆句】What does solidarity entail according to the passage? 标准疑问句，谓语动词 entail 是\n关键考词，\"according to the passage\" 为状语，结构简单。\n【定位】Solidarity is also viewed as a reaction to the extreme emphasis on individualism and freedom\nof choice.\nsolidarity 也被视为对极端强调个人主义与选择自由的一种反应（反对）。\n【选项】A) 张冠李戴\nA limit to personal differences. 来自 restricts expression and practice of individual\ndifferences，那是讲社群主义的代价，不是讲 solidarity 整体的内涵；题干问的是\nsolidarity entail 什么。\nB) ✓ 正确\na reaction to the extreme emphasis on individualism ↔ An opposition to individualism\nthe extreme emphasis on individualism and freedom of choice ↔ individualism\nAn opposition to individualism 是 a reaction to the extreme emphasis on\nindividualism 最直接的同义改写，与预判一致。\nC) 张冠李戴\nA hindrance to social advancement. 把 solidarity 说成\"社会进步的障碍\"，但原文\nsee society as a hindrance to their freedom to make choices 是说\"自由派认为社会\n是其自由的障碍\"，hindrance 的主语应为 society 而非 solidarity；并且 P7 明说\nsolidarity 是 order and progress 的关键。\nD) 过度推断\nAn inevitable end to liberal pursuits. 说 solidarity 会\"不可避免地终结\"自由派追求，\n但原文只说它是对极端个人主义的 reaction（反对/回应），并未说会\"终结\"或必然成\n功，且 inevitable 加重了语气。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-12-2-r-50", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 50, "stem": "What do social theorists think of solidarity?", "options": ["A) It is especially relevant to the most vulnerable.", "B) It is most effective in protecting human dignity.", "C) It is essential to progress and order in society.", "D) It is indispensable to avoiding shallow relationships."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】What do social theorists think of solidarity? 问的是 social theorists 这一具体群体的看\n法，定位明确，不是整篇作者主旨。\n【拆句】What do social theorists think of solidarity? 主语 \"social theorists\"，think of 是固定搭\n配\"看待/认为\"，结构平铺直叙。\n【定位】But despite its many facets, social theorists have viewed solidarity as a key factor in bringing\nabout order and progress in society.\n但是，尽管 solidarity 有诸多面向，社会理论家们仍把它视为推动社会秩序与进步的关键\n因素。\n【选项】A) 以偏概全\nIt is especially relevant to the most vulnerable. 取自 listens to and acts on the\ncries of the most vulnerable，原文这是对 solidarity 内涵的一种延伸描述，\n且\"especially relevant\"是干扰项的额外断言；题干问的是社会理论家对 solidarity 的整\n体看法，而非\"它最关乎谁\"。\nB) 过度推断\nIt is most effective in protecting human dignity. 用 most effective（最有效）做绝\n对化判断，但原文 It fights for the protection of human dignity 仅说它\"为人类尊严\n而战\"，并未与其他事物比较\"最有效\"。\nC) ✓ 正确\na key factor in bringing about order and progress in society ↔\nessential to progress and order in society\nbringing about order and progress in society ↔ progress and order in society\nessential to progress and order in society 是 a key factor in bringing about order\nand progress in society 的标准同义改写，与预判完全对应。\nD) 偷换概念\nIt is indispensable to avoiding shallow relationships. 说 solidarity 对\"避免肤浅关\n系\"必不可少，但原文 they regard a genuine solidarity as more than just shallow\nrelationships based on common interests 是说\"真正的 solidarity 不仅仅是肤浅关\n系\"，是在界定 solidarity 本身，而非说它用于\"避免肤浅关系\"。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-12-2-r-51", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 51, "stem": "What is the finding of one study about people who keep giving unsolicited advice?", "options": ["A) They turn out to be well-meaning.", "B) They are genuinely kind-hearted.", "C) They are capable of influencing others.", "D) They are inclined to crave for power."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】题干 What is the finding of one study 指向某个具体研究的事实性结论，不\n含 infer/imply/mainly。\n【拆句】题干主干是 What is the finding of one study，about 引导的介词短语修饰 finding，限\n定研究对象是 people who keep giving unsolicited advice（who 引导定语从句修饰\npeople）。结构平铺直叙，无陷阱。\n【定位】One study found that people with a high tendency to seek power were more likely to give\nadvice than those with an opposite tendency.\n一项研究发现，求权欲望强的人比求权欲望相反（低）的人更倾向于给出建议。\n【选项】A) 答非所问\nThey turn out to be well-meaning 取自首句 can be well-meaning，但那只是 give\nadvice 的一种可能动机的铺垫，并非“研究的发现”。\nB) 答非所问\nThey are genuinely kind-hearted 改写自 genuinely want to help，同样位于铺垫\n句，不是研究结论。\nC) 偷换概念\nThey are capable of influencing others 把 self-worth around the ability to\ninfluence others 中“自我价值感来自影响他人的能力”误读为“具备影响他人能力”，且这\n同样不在 study 结论里。\nD) ✓ 正确\na high tendency to seek power ↔ inclined to crave for power\n四项中只有 inclined to crave for power 与预判“求权欲强者更爱给建议”形成 1:1 同义\n对应。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-12-2-r-52", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 52, "stem": "What is the problem with people who are driven to give you unsolicited advice?", "options": ["A) They may be quick to upset others.", "B) They may be emotionally vulnerable.", "C) They want their voice to be the loudest.", "D) They respond to discomfort excessively."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】题干 What is the problem with 指向一个可在原文中找到具体描述的缺陷。\n【拆句】主干 What is the problem，介词短语 with people who are driven to give you\nunsolicited advice 修饰 problem；who 引导被动语态定语从句。注意 driven 是被动，表\n示“被某种力量驱使”，而不是主动选择。\n【定位】These people may also display a problematic degree of emotional vulnerability, becoming\nupset very quickly, and taking a long time to calm down.\n这些人还可能表现出某种程度上有问题的情绪脆弱性——很快就情绪激动，且久久不能\n平复。\n【选项】A) 张冠李戴\nThey may be quick to upset others 把原文中“自己易激动”扭曲为“使他人易激动”——\n动作发出者由 these people 变成对他人施加影响。\nB) ✓ 正确\na problematic degree of emotional vulnerability ↔ emotionally vulnerable\n预判的“情绪脆弱”在四项中只与 emotionally vulnerable 对应；A/C/D 都来自定位句的\n修饰部分。\nC) 偷换概念\nThey want their voice to be the loudest 改自 their emotions were only validated\nin childhood when they were at their loudest，那是对成因的猜测且发生在童年，并\n非“现在的问题”本身。\nD) 答非所问\nThey respond to discomfort excessively 取自 adopt responses to discomfort that\nare excessive in most situations，那是脆弱情绪导致的“反应方式”，是表现/后果，而\n题干问的是“问题本身”。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-12-2-r-53", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 53, "stem": "What is the advice receiver tempted to do when noticing the advice giver's underlying anxiety?", "options": ["A) Reveal the undesired result of their approach.", "B) Refuse their advice in an undisguised manner.", "C) Bring their real motives to the surface.", "D) Accuse them of trying to control others."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】题干 What is the advice receiver tempted to do 指向一个原文中可直接对应的具体\n行为。\n【拆句】主干 What is the advice receiver tempted to do，when 引导时间状语从句 when\nnoticing the advice giver’s underlying anxiety（noticing 为分词短语）。注意 tempted\nto do 不等于“已经做”，而是“被诱使想去做”。\n【定位】It can be tempting in this situation to react harshly to the advice giver and to accuse them of\nbeing manipulative, but this approach might produce an undesired result.\n在这种情况下，人很容易被诱使去强硬回怼劝告者并指责他们在操控自己，但这种做法可能\n导致不希望出现的结果。\n【选项】A) 无中生有\nReveal the undesired result of their approach 把原文 this approach might\nproduce an undesired result 中“己方做法的结果”硬塞为“揭露对方做法的结果”，且\nreveal 这一动作原文从未出现。\nB) 张冠李戴\nRefuse their advice in an undisguised manner 取自 an outright rejection 的\noutright（直率的），属于 Q54 区间内容；与 Q53 问的“被诱使做什么”无关。\nC) 无中生有\nBring their real motives to the surface 谈“把真实动机暴露出来”，原文虽提到 They\nmay not be fully aware of this drive，但从未说被诱使去把动机带到台面上。\nD) ✓ 正确\naccuse them of being manipulative ↔ Accuse them of trying to control others\n在 to react harshly 与 to accuse them of being manipulative 两个候选动作里，只有\nAccuse them of trying to control others 与后者形成精确同义改写。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-12-2-r-54", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 54, "stem": "How is the advice giver likely to react to the rejection of their advice?", "options": ["A) They may regard it as a threat.", "B) They may modify their behavior.", "C) They get ready for a larger conflict.", "D) They begin to doubt their self-worth."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】题干 How is the advice giver likely to react 问的是一个原文中有具体描述的反应方式。\n【拆句】主干 How is the advice giver likely to react，to 引导的介词短语 to the rejection of\ntheir advice 表反应对象。likely to 是预测性短语，对应原文的 may/possibly 等情态\n表达。\n【定位】If the act of giving advice is contributing actively to someone's feelings of self-worth, an\noutright rejection may be perceived as a threat, activating their fight-or-flight response,\npossibly causing them to double down on their validation-seeking behavior, or leading to a\nlarger conflict.\n如果给建议这一行为正主动地构建着对方的自我价值感，那么一次直率的拒绝可能被对方视\n为威胁，激活其“战或逃”反应，可能导致他们加倍寻求认可，或引发更大的冲突。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nmay be perceived as a threat ↔ They may regard it as a threat\n预判“视为威胁”在四项中只与 They may regard it as a threat 形成 1:1 同义对应；\nB/C/D 均取自定位句末尾的分词链或脱离原文。\nB) 与原文相反\nThey may modify their behavior 暗示劝告者会调整行为；原文却是 double down on\ntheir validation-seeking behavior——加倍重复原行为，方向相反。\nC) 过度推断\nThey get ready for a larger conflict 描述劝告者“准备好”打更大架；原文是\npossibly...leading to a larger conflict，只是分词链中可能的下游结果，并非劝告者主\n动准备。\nD) 与原文相反\nThey begin to doubt their self-worth 说劝告者开始怀疑自己；原文方向相反——他\n们的 self-worth 被威胁后，会更努力地寻求认可（double down on validation-\nseeking），而不是怀疑。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-12-2-r-55", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 55, "stem": "What should the advice receiver do to avoid unnecessary conflict?", "options": ["A) Concede the advice is perfectly reasonable.", "B) Give their reasons for not taking the advice.", "C) Understand why the advice giver offers the advice.", "D) Explain their chosen way of handling the situation."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】题干 What should the advice receiver do 指向作者给出的明确建议，文章末段往往有\n归纳。\n【拆句】主干 What should the advice receiver do，to 引导目的状语 to avoid unnecessary\nconflict。should 是关键情态动词，意味着答案对应文中明确的建议性陈述，而非可能性\n陈述。\n【定位】It is best to set boundaries in a way that takes into account the individual's underlying reasons\nfor doing what they are doing to avoid unnecessary conflict and more stress.\n最好以一种顾及当事人行为背后深层动机的方式来设界限，以避免不必要的冲突和更多\n压力。\n【选项】A) 张冠李戴\nConcede the advice is perfectly reasonable 让步承认建议合理，是放弃自己立场；原\n文是劝考者“自己设界限是 perfectly reasonable”，主语完全不同。\nB) 偷换概念\nGive their reasons for not taking the advice 让 receiver 说出自己的拒绝理由；原文\n范本话术 I have my own plan for handling this 谈的是“我自有方案”，并未要求陈述\n拒绝理由，更不是 P9 给出的总策略。\nC) ✓ 正确\ntakes into account the individual's underlying reasons for doing what they are doing ↔\nUnderstand why the advice giver offers the advice\n预判“顾及对方深层动机”在四项中只与 Understand why the advice giver offers the\nadvice 形成 1:1 同义对应。\nD) 以偏概全\nExplain their chosen way of handling the situation 描述 receiver 解释自己的处理方\n式，对应 I have my own plan for handling this 的示范话术之一，但题干问的是“避免\n冲突的做法”——文末 P9 的总结才是答案。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}]});
